export const MATHS = [
{"id":"LW-H01","lv":"sale","d":4,"area":"Selling goods","q":"Under Incoterms 2020 FOB, risk passes from seller to buyer when the goods are","o":["handed to the first carrier at the seller's premises","placed on board the vessel at the port of loading","unloaded onto the quay at the port of discharge","cleared for export and ready at the terminal gate"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Since 2010 it is on board, not 'over the ship's rail'. The buyer books the ship and insures the voyage, and ownership may pass later.","about":["fob"]},
{"id":"LW-H02","lv":"sale","d":5,"area":"Selling goods","q":"A CIF seller must insure the cargo for at least","o":["100% of the contract price","105% of the contract price","110% of the contract price","120% of the contract price"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Incoterms 2020: 110% of the price in the contract, in the contract's currency, with the buyer able to claim under the policy. A buyer who wants more must ask for it.","about":["cif"]},
{"id":"LW-H03","lv":"sale","d":6,"area":"Selling goods","q":"Incoterms 2020 raised the insurance a CIP seller must buy to Institute Cargo Clauses (A). For CIF the minimum stays","o":["Institute Cargo Clauses (A)","Institute Cargo Clauses (B)","Institute Cargo Clauses (C)","Institute War Clauses (Cargo)"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Clauses (C), the shortest list of named perils. The ICC kept it for CIF because the term is much used for bulk commodities, where cheap cover is the custom; CIP, used more for manufactured goods, went to all risks.","about":["cif","incoterms"]},
{"id":"LW-H04","lv":"sale","d":4,"area":"Selling goods","q":"Which of these Incoterms 2020 can be used for any mode of transport, not only sea?","o":["FOB","CFR","CIF","FCA"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"FOB, CFR, CIF and FAS are for sea and inland waterways only. A container handed over at an inland terminal is delivered long before it's on board, so FCA suits it better than FOB.","about":["incoterms"]},
{"id":"LW-H05","lv":"sale","d":5,"area":"Selling goods","q":"Incoterms 2020 renamed DAT (delivered at terminal) as","o":["DAP, delivered at place","DDU, delivered duty unpaid","DPU, delivered at place unloaded","DAF, delivered at frontier"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"DAT could only be a terminal; DPU can be any place, with the goods always unloaded by the seller, which is what sets it apart from DAP.","about":["incoterms"]},
{"id":"LW-H07","lv":"sale","d":4,"area":"Selling goods","q":"A buyer under a CIF contract must pay","o":["once the cargo has arrived and passed inspection","on tender of the documents, even if the cargo has been lost","on tender of the documents, unless the cargo has been lost","once the ship has berthed at the port of discharge"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"A CIF sale is a sale of documents: the seller performs by tendering the bill of lading, the policy and the invoice, and the buyer's remedy for a loss is the insurance (Manbre Saccharine v Corn Products, 1919).","about":["cif"]},
{"id":"LW-H10","lv":"finance","d":4,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"Under UCP 600, a bank has at most how long to examine the documents presented under a credit?","o":["three banking days after the day of presentation","seven banking days after the day of presentation","five banking days after the day of presentation","ten banking days after the day of presentation"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Article 14(b). UCP 500 allowed a reasonable time up to seven banking days; UCP 600 (2007) cut it to five, and a notice of refusal must go out within the same time.","about":["letter-of-credit"]},
{"id":"LW-H11","lv":"finance","d":5,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"Under UCP 600 a documentary credit is","o":["revocable until the goods ship, then irrevocable","irrevocable: no one can amend or cancel it alone","revocable until a confirming bank adds its promise","irrevocable only once the applicant approves the documents"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Article 2 defines a credit as irrevocable by its nature; the revocable credit of UCP 500 went in 2007, and an amendment needs the issuing bank, any confirming bank and the beneficiary (art. 10).","about":["letter-of-credit"]},
{"id":"LW-H12","lv":"finance","d":5,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"Unless the credit says otherwise, UCP 600 requires the documents to be presented no later than","o":["14 calendar days after shipment","30 calendar days after shipment","10 banking days after shipment","21 calendar days after shipment"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Article 14(c), and never later than the credit's expiry. Late presentation is among the commonest reasons a bank refuses documents.","about":["letter-of-credit"]},
{"id":"LW-H14","lv":"shipping","d":4,"area":"Ships and cargo","q":"A bill of lading is a receipt for the cargo, evidence of the contract of carriage and","o":["a guarantee of payment, issued by the buyer's bank","a certificate of origin, accepted by customs at import","a document of title, which can pass the cargo by endorsement","a policy of insurance, paying if the cargo is lost"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Being a document of title is what lets a cargo be sold afloat, and what lets a bank hold it as security: whoever holds an original can claim the goods.","about":["bill-of-lading"]},
{"id":"LW-H22","lv":"contract","d":4,"area":"Contracts","q":"A contract made under economic duress is","o":["void from the start, as if never made","valid, but the victim may claim damages","valid, but unenforceable for the excess","voidable by the victim, if it acts promptly"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"The victim may affirm it or set it aside, and delay loses the right: in The Atlantic Baron (1979) a shipbuilder's threat was enough but the owners had waited too long.","about":["economic-duress"]},
{"id":"LW-H23","lv":"disputes","d":3,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"A claim for breach of a simple contract must be brought in England within","o":["one year of the breach","three years of the breach","twelve years of the breach","six years of the breach"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Limitation Act 1980, s 5. A deed gets twelve years (s 8). A written acknowledgement of a debt starts the clock again.","about":["limitation-period"]},
{"id":"LW-H24","lv":"disputes","d":4,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"An arbitral award can be enforced under the New York Convention in how many states?","o":["about 120","more than 170","about 90","fewer than 50"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"That reach is why commodity disputes go to arbitration: a court judgment has the 2019 Hague Judgments Convention and a patchwork of treaties. The grounds for refusing an award are listed in article V.","about":["new-york-convention"]},
{"id":"LW-H27","lv":"wrongs","d":2,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"In Donoghue v Stevenson (1932), the claimant fell ill after finding what in her bottle of ginger beer?","o":["a dead mouse","a shard of glass","a decomposed snail","a drowned wasp"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Lord Atkin's neighbour principle: take reasonable care to avoid acts you can reasonably foresee would injure your neighbour. The case was decided on the assumption that the snail was there; it was never proved.","about":["duty-of-care"]},
{"id":"LW-H28","lv":"companies","d":4,"area":"Companies and agents","q":"In Salomon v A Salomon & Co, Mr Salomon had lent his company money against a debenture. The House of Lords held that","o":["he was liable for its debts, being its real owner","the company was a separate person, so he was paid first","the debenture was void as he held nearly all the shares","the company was a sham, so all creditors shared equally"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"The company was a person apart from its owner, and as a secured creditor Mr Salomon came ahead of the unsecured ones. The principle has been trimmed (Prest v Petrodel, 2013) but is the root of limited liability.","about":["separate-legal-personality"]},
{"id":"LW-H29","lv":"states","d":3,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"Under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea, a coastal state's territorial sea reaches","o":["3 nautical miles from the coast","24 nautical miles from the coast","12 nautical miles from the coast","200 nautical miles from the coast"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Article 3. In the territorial sea foreign ships have a right of innocent passage. The contiguous zone reaches 24 miles and the exclusive economic zone 200.","about":["mare-liberum"]},
{"id":"LW-rule-of-law","lv":"states","d":3,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"What is meant by “Rule of law”?","o":["everyone, the state included, answers to known laws in the courts","the courts may make new law whenever parliament has stayed silent on it","a law passed by a majority binds even those who voted against","every law must first be approved by the people in a referendum"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Rule of law: everyone, the state included, answers to known laws in the courts. Dicey's three senses (1885): no punishment but for a breach of law proved in court, everyone equal before the ordinary law, rights secured by the courts. Magna Carta's clause 39 is its oldest root.","about":["rule-of-law"],"facts":["rule-of-law.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-rule-of-law-name","lv":"states","d":4,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"Which legal term means this? “Everyone, the state included, answers to known laws in the courts”","o":["Conversion","Frustration","But-for test","Rule of law"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Rule of law: everyone, the state included, answers to known laws in the courts. Dicey's three senses (1885): no punishment but for a breach of law proved in court, everyone equal before the ordinary law, rights secured by the courts. Magna Carta's clause 39 is its oldest root.","about":["rule-of-law"],"facts":["rule-of-law.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-separation-of-powers","lv":"states","d":3,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"What is meant by “Separation of powers”?","o":["the central government and the regions each rule in their own sphere","law-making, governing and judging are kept in different hands","the state and the church keep out of each other's affairs","the courts may strike down any law that breaks the constitution"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Separation of powers: law-making, governing and judging are kept in different hands. Montesquieu, The Spirit of the Laws (1748), drawing on England, and written into the US Constitution's three articles. Britain's own is looser: ministers sit in Parliament, and the Supreme Court has sat apart from the Lords only since 2009.","about":["separation-of-powers"],"facts":["separation-of-powers.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-separation-of-powers-name","lv":"states","d":4,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"Which legal term means this? “Law-making, governing and judging are kept in different hands”","o":["Rule of law","Anticipatory breach","Specific performance","Separation of powers"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Separation of powers: law-making, governing and judging are kept in different hands. Montesquieu, The Spirit of the Laws (1748), drawing on England, and written into the US Constitution's three articles. Britain's own is looser: ministers sit in Parliament, and the Supreme Court has sat apart from the Lords only since 2009.","about":["separation-of-powers"],"facts":["separation-of-powers.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-trust","lv":"property","d":3,"area":"Property and equity","q":"What is meant by “Trust”?","o":["property held by one person for the benefit of another","property owned in shares by several people at once","property pledged to a lender until a debt is repaid","property lent to another, to be returned in the same kind"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Trust: property held by one person for the benefit of another. Equity's great invention: the trustee holds the legal title, the beneficiary the benefit, and trust property is safe from the trustee's creditors. A Quistclose trust (Barclays Bank v Quistclose, 1970) keeps money lent for one purpose apart if the borrower fails.","about":["trust"],"facts":["trust.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-trust-name","lv":"property","d":4,"area":"Property and equity","q":"Which legal term means this? “Property held by one person for the benefit of another”","o":["Adverse possession","Bona fide purchaser","Pledge","Trust"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Trust: property held by one person for the benefit of another. Equity's great invention: the trustee holds the legal title, the beneficiary the benefit, and trust property is safe from the trustee's creditors. A Quistclose trust (Barclays Bank v Quistclose, 1970) keeps money lent for one purpose apart if the borrower fails.","about":["trust"],"facts":["trust.branch","trust.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-bailment","lv":"property","d":5,"area":"Property and equity","q":"What is meant by “Bailment”?","o":["a charge on goods registered as security for a lender","possession of goods given to another without giving up ownership","release of a prisoner before trial against a sum of money","ownership of goods given to another while keeping possession of them"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Bailment: possession of goods given to another without giving up ownership. A carrier, a warehouse, a tank terminal: each holds as bailee, bound to take reasonable care and to deliver to the bailor or its order. Oil of several owners mixed in one tank is owned in common, each by its share (Indian Oil v Greenstone Shipping, 1988).","about":["bailment"],"facts":["bailment.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-bailment-name","lv":"property","d":6,"area":"Property and equity","q":"Which legal term means this? “Possession of goods given to another without giving up ownership”","o":["Incoterms","Set-off","Trust","Bailment"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Bailment: possession of goods given to another without giving up ownership. A carrier, a warehouse, a tank terminal: each holds as bailee, bound to take reasonable care and to deliver to the bailor or its order. Oil of several owners mixed in one tank is owned in common, each by its share (Indian Oil v Greenstone Shipping, 1988).","about":["bailment"],"facts":["bailment.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-duty-of-care","lv":"wrongs","d":3,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What is meant by “Duty of care”?","o":["the obligation to look after goods you hold for someone else","the obligation to rescue anyone you see in danger, if you safely can","the obligation to tell a buyer of every defect you know of","the obligation to avoid acts you can foresee would harm your neighbour"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Duty of care: the obligation to avoid acts you can foresee would harm your neighbour. Donoghue v Stevenson (1932): a snail in a bottle of ginger beer, and Lord Atkin's 'neighbour', anyone so closely and directly affected that you ought to have them in mind. Caparo v Dickman (1990) asks foreseeability, proximity and whether a duty is fair.","about":["duty-of-care"],"facts":["duty-of-care.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-duty-of-care-name","lv":"wrongs","d":4,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “The obligation to avoid acts you can foresee would harm your neighbour”","o":["Contributory negligence","Duty of care","But-for test","Penalty rule"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Duty of care: the obligation to avoid acts you can foresee would harm your neighbour. Donoghue v Stevenson (1932): a snail in a bottle of ginger beer, and Lord Atkin's 'neighbour', anyone so closely and directly affected that you ought to have them in mind. Caparo v Dickman (1990) asks foreseeability, proximity and whether a duty is fair.","about":["duty-of-care"],"facts":["duty-of-care.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-contributory-negligence","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What is meant by “Contributory negligence”?","o":["damages are cut if the claimant didn't try to limit its loss","damages are cut by the claimant's own share in causing its harm","damages are refused if the claimant knowingly accepted the risk","damages are shared equally between two wrongdoers of one harm"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Contributory negligence: damages are cut by the claimant's own share in causing its harm. Since the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945 a reduction 'as the court thinks just and equitable', where before it was a complete defence. A car passenger without a seat belt typically loses a quarter or less (Froom v Butcher, 1976).","about":["contributory-negligence"],"facts":["contributory-negligence.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-contributory-negligence-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “Damages are cut by the claimant's own share in causing its harm”","o":["Contributory negligence","Duty of care","Anticipatory breach","Separation of powers"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Contributory negligence: damages are cut by the claimant's own share in causing its harm. Since the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945 a reduction 'as the court thinks just and equitable', where before it was a complete defence. A car passenger without a seat belt typically loses a quarter or less (Froom v Butcher, 1976).","about":["contributory-negligence"],"facts":["contributory-negligence.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-misrepresentation","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Misrepresentation”?","o":["a failure to mention a fact the other side would have liked to know","an honest opinion, given by an expert, that turned out wrong","a promise about the future that wasn't kept, however honest","a false statement of fact that induced the other side to contract"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Misrepresentation: a false statement of fact that induced the other side to contract. It lets the misled party rescind and, under the Misrepresentation Act 1967, s 2(1), claim damages as if for fraud unless the maker proves it reasonably believed the statement true. Silence isn't a misrepresentation, except in insurance and the like.","about":["misrepresentation"],"facts":["misrepresentation.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-misrepresentation-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “A false statement of fact that induced the other side to contract”","o":["Consideration","Economic duress","Invitation to treat","Misrepresentation"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Misrepresentation: a false statement of fact that induced the other side to contract. It lets the misled party rescind and, under the Misrepresentation Act 1967, s 2(1), claim damages as if for fraud unless the maker proves it reasonably believed the statement true. Silence isn't a misrepresentation, except in insurance and the like.","about":["misrepresentation"],"facts":["misrepresentation.branch","misrepresentation.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-frustration","lv":"contract","d":3,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Frustration”?","o":["an event no one foresaw makes performance impossible or radically different","an event no one foresaw makes performance dearer, so the price may be raised","an event no one foresaw lets the party it hurts suspend the deal awhile","an event the parties foresaw and named excuses whoever it hits"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Frustration: an event no one foresaw makes performance impossible or radically different. Taylor v Caldwell (1863): the music hall burnt down. Narrow: closing the Suez Canal in 1956 didn't frustrate a CIF sale of groundnuts, shipping round the Cape being merely dearer (Tsakiroglou v Noblee Thorl, 1962). The contract ends, and the Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943 settles the money.","about":["frustration"],"facts":["frustration.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-frustration-name","lv":"contract","d":4,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “An event no one foresaw makes performance impossible or radically different”","o":["Consideration","Penalty rule","Misrepresentation","Frustration"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Frustration: an event no one foresaw makes performance impossible or radically different. Taylor v Caldwell (1863): the music hall burnt down. Narrow: closing the Suez Canal in 1956 didn't frustrate a CIF sale of groundnuts, shipping round the Cape being merely dearer (Tsakiroglou v Noblee Thorl, 1962). The contract ends, and the Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943 settles the money.","about":["frustration"],"facts":["frustration.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-specific-performance","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Specific performance”?","o":["a court order to pay a fixed sum agreed in advance for breach","a court order to return what each side received under the deal","a court order to stop doing something the contract forbids","a court order to do what was promised, not merely to pay damages"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Specific performance: a court order to do what was promised, not merely to pay damages. Discretionary in English law, given only where damages won't do: land, a unique thing, rarely ordinary goods (Sale of Goods Act 1979, s 52). In German and French law it's the primary remedy.","about":["specific-performance"],"facts":["specific-performance.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-specific-performance-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “A court order to do what was promised, not merely to pay damages”","o":["Specific performance","Implied terms","Anticipatory breach","Privity of contract"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Specific performance: a court order to do what was promised, not merely to pay damages. Discretionary in English law, given only where damages won't do: land, a unique thing, rarely ordinary goods (Sale of Goods Act 1979, s 52). In German and French law it's the primary remedy.","about":["specific-performance"],"facts":["specific-performance.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-mitigation","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Mitigation”?","o":["a claimant can't recover loss it could reasonably have avoided","a claimant's damages are cut by its own share of blame for the loss","a claimant must give notice of its loss before it can recover","a claimant may recover the cost of a substitute, however high"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Mitigation: a claimant can't recover loss it could reasonably have avoided. British Westinghouse v Underground Electric Railways (1912). In a sale, a seller who fails to deliver owes the difference between the contract and the market price, because the buyer should buy in the market (Sale of Goods Act 1979, s 51(3)).","about":["mitigation"],"facts":["mitigation.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-mitigation-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “A claimant can't recover loss it could reasonably have avoided”","o":["Penalty rule","Misrepresentation","Postal rule","Mitigation"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Mitigation: a claimant can't recover loss it could reasonably have avoided. British Westinghouse v Underground Electric Railways (1912). In a sale, a seller who fails to deliver owes the difference between the contract and the market price, because the buyer should buy in the market (Sale of Goods Act 1979, s 51(3)).","about":["mitigation"],"facts":["mitigation.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-anticipatory-breach","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Anticipatory breach”?","o":["a clear refusal to perform, made well before performance falls due","a warning that performance may be late, made before the date","a failure to perform on the date it fell due, without excuse","a breach the other side forgives by carrying on with the deal"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Anticipatory breach: a clear refusal to perform, made well before performance falls due. Hochster v De la Tour (1853): the innocent party may accept it and sue at once, or keep the contract alive and wait, at the risk that something meanwhile frees the other side (Avery v Bowden, 1855: the Crimean War closed Odessa).","about":["anticipatory-breach"],"facts":["anticipatory-breach.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-anticipatory-breach-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “A clear refusal to perform, made well before performance falls due”","o":["Misrepresentation","Implied terms","Consideration","Anticipatory breach"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Anticipatory breach: a clear refusal to perform, made well before performance falls due. Hochster v De la Tour (1853): the innocent party may accept it and sue at once, or keep the contract alive and wait, at the risk that something meanwhile frees the other side (Avery v Bowden, 1855: the Crimean War closed Odessa).","about":["anticipatory-breach"],"facts":["anticipatory-breach.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-penalty-rule","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Penalty rule”?","o":["a sum fixed for breach isn't enforced if out of all proportion","a sum fixed for breach goes to the court, not to the injured party","a sum fixed for breach is enforced only if it equals the real loss","a sum fixed for breach is always enforced, being freely agreed"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Penalty rule: a sum fixed for breach isn't enforced if out of all proportion. Cavendish Square v Makdessi and ParkingEye v Beavis (2015) replaced the old test, a genuine pre-estimate of loss (Dunlop v New Garage, 1915), with legitimate interest and proportion. French law lets a judge reduce an excessive sum instead (Code civil, art. 1231-5).","about":["penalty-rule"],"facts":["penalty-rule.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-penalty-rule-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “A sum fixed for breach isn't enforced if out of all proportion”","o":["Mitigation","Misrepresentation","Postal rule","Penalty rule"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Penalty rule: a sum fixed for breach isn't enforced if out of all proportion. Cavendish Square v Makdessi and ParkingEye v Beavis (2015) replaced the old test, a genuine pre-estimate of loss (Dunlop v New Garage, 1915), with legitimate interest and proportion. French law lets a judge reduce an excessive sum instead (Code civil, art. 1231-5).","about":["penalty-rule"],"facts":["penalty-rule.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-limitation-period","lv":"disputes","d":5,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What is meant by “Limitation period”?","o":["the cap on damages a party can claim under the contract","the time allowed to bring a claim, after which it's barred","the time a buyer has to reject goods once they're delivered","the time a court has to give judgment after the hearing"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Limitation period: the time allowed to bring a claim, after which it's barred. In England six years for contract and tort and twelve for a deed (Limitation Act 1980); three years in Germany (§ 195 BGB) and five in France, from when the claimant knew. Cargo claims under the Hague-Visby Rules: one year.","about":["limitation-period"],"facts":["limitation-period.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-limitation-period-name","lv":"disputes","d":6,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “The time allowed to bring a claim, after which it's barred”","o":["Misrepresentation","Letter of credit","Without prejudice","Limitation period"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Limitation period: the time allowed to bring a claim, after which it's barred. In England six years for contract and tort and twelve for a deed (Limitation Act 1980); three years in Germany (§ 195 BGB) and five in France, from when the claimant knew. Cargo claims under the Hague-Visby Rules: one year.","about":["limitation-period"],"facts":["limitation-period.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-adverse-possession","lv":"property","d":5,"area":"Property and equity","q":"What is meant by “Adverse possession”?","o":["title gained by buying land in good faith from a non-owner","title gained by holding land openly as if owner for long enough","title gained by the state over land whose owner can't be found","title gained by a tenant who has paid rent for twenty years"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Adverse possession: title gained by holding land openly as if owner for long enough. Twelve years for unregistered land in England (Limitation Act 1980); for registered land, ten years and then an application the owner can usually defeat (Land Registration Act 2002). Roman law's usucapio; in Germany, ten years for movables (§ 937 BGB).","about":["adverse-possession"],"facts":["adverse-possession.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-adverse-possession-name","lv":"property","d":6,"area":"Property and equity","q":"Which legal term means this? “Title gained by holding land openly as if owner for long enough”","o":["Invitation to treat","Insurable interest","Trust","Adverse possession"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Adverse possession: title gained by holding land openly as if owner for long enough. Twelve years for unregistered land in England (Limitation Act 1980); for registered land, ten years and then an application the owner can usually defeat (Land Registration Act 2002). Roman law's usucapio; in Germany, ten years for movables (§ 937 BGB).","about":["adverse-possession"],"facts":["adverse-possession.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-bona-fide-purchaser","lv":"property","d":7,"area":"Property and equity","q":"What is meant by “Bona fide purchaser”?","o":["a buyer at a public auction takes free of any claim to the goods sold","a buyer who pays full value is always safe from the true owner","a buyer acting in good faith may keep goods later found stolen","a buyer for value without notice takes free of equitable claims"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Bona fide purchaser: a buyer for value without notice takes free of equitable claims. 'Equity's darling': a purchaser of the legal title for value without notice defeats earlier equitable interests, such as a beneficiary's under a trust. Notice includes what proper inquiries would have shown.","about":["bona-fide-purchaser"],"facts":["bona-fide-purchaser.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-bona-fide-purchaser-name","lv":"property","d":8,"area":"Property and equity","q":"Which legal term means this? “A buyer for value without notice takes free of equitable claims”","o":["Adverse possession","Privity of contract","Bona fide purchaser","New York Convention"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Bona fide purchaser: a buyer for value without notice takes free of equitable claims. 'Equity's darling': a purchaser of the legal title for value without notice defeats earlier equitable interests, such as a beneficiary's under a trust. Notice includes what proper inquiries would have shown.","about":["bona-fide-purchaser"],"facts":["bona-fide-purchaser.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-fiduciary-duty","lv":"companies","d":5,"area":"Companies and agents","q":"What is meant by “Fiduciary duty”?","o":["a duty to take the care a reasonable person would in the job","a duty of loyalty: no conflicts and no secret profit","a duty to carry out every lawful instruction of the board","a duty to keep the company's affairs secret from its rivals"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Fiduciary duty: a duty of loyalty: no conflicts and no secret profit. Owed by trustees, directors, agents and partners: Keech v Sandford (1726) and Regal (Hastings) v Gulliver (1942) strip a fiduciary of its profit even where the principal lost nothing. Directors' duties are now in the Companies Act 2006, ss 171 to 177.","about":["fiduciary-duty"],"facts":["fiduciary-duty.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-fiduciary-duty-name","lv":"companies","d":6,"area":"Companies and agents","q":"Which legal term means this? “A duty of loyalty: no conflicts and no secret profit”","o":["Consideration","Bill of lading","Fiduciary duty","Implied terms"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Fiduciary duty: a duty of loyalty: no conflicts and no secret profit. Owed by trustees, directors, agents and partners: Keech v Sandford (1726) and Regal (Hastings) v Gulliver (1942) strip a fiduciary of its profit even where the principal lost nothing. Directors' duties are now in the Companies Act 2006, ss 171 to 177.","about":["fiduciary-duty"],"facts":["fiduciary-duty.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-separate-legal-personality","lv":"companies","d":3,"area":"Companies and agents","q":"What is meant by “Separate legal personality”?","o":["a company may act only in the ways its founding papers list","a company's directors own its assets on its members' behalf","a company has rights and debts of its own, apart from its owners","a company's owners answer for its debts up to their own wealth"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Separate legal personality: a company has rights and debts of its own, apart from its owners. Salomon v A Salomon & Co (House of Lords, 1896; reported 1897): a one-man leather business was a company of its own, so its founder, a secured creditor, was paid ahead of the others. The veil is lifted only where a company is used to evade an existing obligation (Prest v Petrodel, 2013).","about":["separate-legal-personality"],"facts":["separate-legal-personality.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-separate-legal-personality-name","lv":"companies","d":4,"area":"Companies and agents","q":"Which legal term means this? “A company has rights and debts of its own, apart from its owners”","o":["Anticipatory breach","Bona fide purchaser","Fiduciary duty","Separate legal personality"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Separate legal personality: a company has rights and debts of its own, apart from its owners. Salomon v A Salomon & Co (House of Lords, 1896; reported 1897): a one-man leather business was a company of its own, so its founder, a secured creditor, was paid ahead of the others. The veil is lifted only where a company is used to evade an existing obligation (Prest v Petrodel, 2013).","about":["separate-legal-personality"],"facts":["separate-legal-personality.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-letter-of-credit","lv":"finance","d":3,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"What is meant by “Letter of credit”?","o":["a bank's promise to repay the buyer if the goods prove defective","a bank's promise to collect the price from the buyer for the seller","a bank's promise to lend the buyer the price until it resells","a bank's promise to pay the seller against documents that comply"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Letter of credit: a bank's promise to pay the seller against documents that comply. Governed by the ICC's UCP 600 (2007): an irrevocable undertaking, confirmed if a second bank, usually in the seller's country, adds its own. Banks deal in documents, not goods, and have at most five banking days to examine them.","about":["letter-of-credit"],"facts":["letter-of-credit.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-letter-of-credit-name","lv":"finance","d":4,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"Which legal term means this? “A bank's promise to pay the seller against documents that comply”","o":["Limitation period","Set-off","Misrepresentation","Letter of credit"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Letter of credit: a bank's promise to pay the seller against documents that comply. Governed by the ICC's UCP 600 (2007): an irrevocable undertaking, confirmed if a second bank, usually in the seller's country, adds its own. Banks deal in documents, not goods, and have at most five banking days to examine them.","about":["letter-of-credit"],"facts":["letter-of-credit.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-incoterms","lv":"sale","d":3,"area":"Selling goods","q":"What is meant by “Incoterms”?","o":["the ICC's rules for guarantees payable on first written demand","the ICC's rules for how banks collect payment against shipping documents","the ICC's terms for who ships, clears customs and bears risk","the ICC's rules for how banks examine documents under a credit"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Incoterms: the ICC's terms for who ships, clears customs and bears risk. First published in 1936; Incoterms 2020 has eleven: seven for any mode of transport (EXW, FCA, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, DDP) and four for sea and inland waterways (FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF). They say nothing about when ownership passes.","about":["incoterms"],"facts":["incoterms.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-incoterms-name","lv":"sale","d":4,"area":"Selling goods","q":"Which legal term means this? “The ICC's terms for who ships, clears customs and bears risk”","o":["Mitigation","Frustration","Incoterms","Conversion"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Incoterms: the ICC's terms for who ships, clears customs and bears risk. First published in 1936; Incoterms 2020 has eleven: seven for any mode of transport (EXW, FCA, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, DDP) and four for sea and inland waterways (FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF). They say nothing about when ownership passes.","about":["incoterms"],"facts":["incoterms.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-fob","lv":"sale","d":3,"area":"Selling goods","q":"What is meant by “FOB”?","o":["the seller books and pays the ship; risk passes once goods are loaded","the seller pays the ship and insurance; risk passes on arrival at port","the buyer books the ship; risk passes once goods are on the quay beside it","the buyer books the ship; risk passes once the goods are loaded aboard"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"FOB: the buyer books the ship; risk passes once the goods are loaded aboard. Free on board, Incoterms 2020: sea and inland waterways only. Risk passes when the goods are on board (the ship's rail went in 2010). In oil and biofuels, FOB means the buyer nominates the ship to arrive within the laycan, the agreed window, and the seller's terminal loads it.","about":["fob"],"facts":["fob.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-fob-name","lv":"sale","d":4,"area":"Selling goods","q":"Which legal term means this? “The buyer books the ship; risk passes once the goods are loaded aboard”","o":["Bailment","Pledge","Conversion","FOB"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"FOB: the buyer books the ship; risk passes once the goods are loaded aboard. Free on board, Incoterms 2020: sea and inland waterways only. Risk passes when the goods are on board (the ship's rail went in 2010). In oil and biofuels, FOB means the buyer nominates the ship to arrive within the laycan, the agreed window, and the seller's terminal loads it.","about":["fob"],"facts":["fob.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-cif","lv":"sale","d":3,"area":"Selling goods","q":"What is meant by “CIF”?","o":["the seller pays freight, insurance and duties; risk passes at delivery","the seller pays freight and insurance; risk passes on arrival","the seller pays the freight but not insurance; risk passes on loading","the seller pays freight and insurance; risk passes on loading"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"CIF: the seller pays freight and insurance; risk passes on loading. Cost, insurance and freight: a sale of documents. The buyer must pay against the bill of lading, the policy and the invoice even if the ship has sunk (Manbre Saccharine v Corn Products, 1919). Minimum cover, Institute Cargo Clauses (C), for 110% of the price.","about":["cif"],"facts":["cif.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-cif-name","lv":"sale","d":4,"area":"Selling goods","q":"Which legal term means this? “The seller pays freight and insurance; risk passes on loading”","o":["Conversion","Bailment","Trust","CIF"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"CIF: the seller pays freight and insurance; risk passes on loading. Cost, insurance and freight: a sale of documents. The buyer must pay against the bill of lading, the policy and the invoice even if the ship has sunk (Manbre Saccharine v Corn Products, 1919). Minimum cover, Institute Cargo Clauses (C), for 110% of the price.","about":["cif"],"facts":["cif.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-bill-of-lading","lv":"shipping","d":3,"area":"Ships and cargo","q":"What is meant by “Bill of lading”?","o":["the carrier's receipt for cargo, which also passes title to it","the charterer's order to the master to load a named cargo","the port's record of what each ship loaded and when it sailed","the carrier's invoice for freight, paid before the cargo is released"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Bill of lading: the carrier's receipt for cargo, which also passes title to it. Receipt for the goods, evidence of the contract of carriage and a document of title (Lickbarrow v Mason, 1794): whoever holds an original can claim the cargo, so it can be sold afloat by endorsement. The UK's Electronic Trade Documents Act 2023 gives an electronic one the same standing.","about":["bill-of-lading"],"facts":["bill-of-lading.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-bill-of-lading-name","lv":"shipping","d":4,"area":"Ships and cargo","q":"Which legal term means this? “The carrier's receipt for cargo, which also passes title to it”","o":["But-for test","Bill of lading","Fiduciary duty","Economic duress"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Bill of lading: the carrier's receipt for cargo, which also passes title to it. Receipt for the goods, evidence of the contract of carriage and a document of title (Lickbarrow v Mason, 1794): whoever holds an original can claim the cargo, so it can be sold afloat by endorsement. The UK's Electronic Trade Documents Act 2023 gives an electronic one the same standing.","about":["bill-of-lading"],"facts":["bill-of-lading.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-new-york-convention","lv":"disputes","d":5,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What is meant by “New York Convention”?","o":["the treaty that set up the UN's court for disputes between states","the treaty under which arbitral awards are enforced around the world","the treaty under which court judgments are enforced around the world","the treaty that founded the ICC's court of arbitration in Paris"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"New York Convention: the treaty under which arbitral awards are enforced around the world. The Convention on the Recognition and Enforcement of Foreign Arbitral Awards (1958), with over 170 states party: an award may be refused only on the narrow grounds of its article V. It's why commodity disputes go to GAFTA, FOSFA, LMAA or ICC arbitration rather than to court.","about":["new-york-convention"],"facts":["new-york-convention.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-new-york-convention-name","lv":"disputes","d":6,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “The treaty under which arbitral awards are enforced around the world”","o":["Without prejudice","Invitation to treat","Bona fide purchaser","New York Convention"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"New York Convention: the treaty under which arbitral awards are enforced around the world. The Convention on the Recognition and Enforcement of Foreign Arbitral Awards (1958), with over 170 states party: an award may be refused only on the narrow grounds of its article V. It's why commodity disputes go to GAFTA, FOSFA, LMAA or ICC arbitration rather than to court.","about":["new-york-convention"],"facts":["new-york-convention.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-without-prejudice","lv":"disputes","d":5,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What is meant by “Without prejudice”?","o":["talks to settle a dispute can't be shown to the court later","a concession made now doesn't stop the party arguing it later","a settlement offer binds the offeror until the other side answers","a judge must hold no earlier view of the parties or the case"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Without prejudice: talks to settle a dispute can't be shown to the court later. Protects genuine attempts to settle (Rush & Tompkins v Greater London Council, 1989), unless there's unambiguous impropriety. 'Without prejudice save as to costs' (a Calderbank offer) can be shown to the judge when costs are decided.","about":["without-prejudice"],"facts":["without-prejudice.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-without-prejudice-name","lv":"disputes","d":6,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “Talks to settle a dispute can't be shown to the court later”","o":["Limitation period","Letter of credit","Adverse possession","Without prejudice"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Without prejudice: talks to settle a dispute can't be shown to the court later. Protects genuine attempts to settle (Rush & Tompkins v Greater London Council, 1989), unless there's unambiguous impropriety. 'Without prejudice save as to costs' (a Calderbank offer) can be shown to the judge when costs are decided.","about":["without-prejudice"],"facts":["without-prejudice.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-subrogation","lv":"finance","d":5,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"What is meant by “Subrogation”?","o":["an insurer that has paid may cancel the policy for the rest of the year","an insurer that has paid may claim a share from the other insurers","an insurer that has paid may raise the premium on renewal","an insurer that has paid takes over the insured's own claims against others"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Subrogation: an insurer that has paid takes over the insured's own claims against others. From Roman law by way of equity: the insurer can't recover more than it paid, and the insured mustn't release the wrongdoer and so lose the insurer its claim. A guarantor who pays is subrogated too.","about":["subrogation"],"facts":["subrogation.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-subrogation-name","lv":"finance","d":6,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"Which legal term means this? “An insurer that has paid takes over the insured's own claims against others”","o":["Frustration","Set-off","Pledge","Subrogation"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Subrogation: an insurer that has paid takes over the insured's own claims against others. From Roman law by way of equity: the insurer can't recover more than it paid, and the insured mustn't release the wrongdoer and so lose the insurer its claim. A guarantor who pays is subrogated too.","about":["subrogation"],"facts":["subrogation.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-insurable-interest","lv":"finance","d":5,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"What is meant by “Insurable interest”?","o":["the share of a loss each insurer pays when two cover one risk","a real stake in the thing covered, so that its loss would harm you","the rate charged for cover, as a share of the sum covered","the part of a loss the insured bears itself, before cover pays"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Insurable interest: a real stake in the thing covered, so that its loss would harm you. Without it a policy is a wager. The Marine Insurance Act 1906 requires it at the time of the loss (s 6); an FOB or CIF buyer has it once the risk has passed, even before it owns the goods.","about":["insurable-interest"],"facts":["insurable-interest.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-insurable-interest-name","lv":"finance","d":6,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"Which legal term means this? “A real stake in the thing covered, so that its loss would harm you”","o":["Anticipatory breach","Adverse possession","Insurable interest","Set-off"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Insurable interest: a real stake in the thing covered, so that its loss would harm you. Without it a policy is a wager. The Marine Insurance Act 1906 requires it at the time of the loss (s 6); an FOB or CIF buyer has it once the risk has passed, even before it owns the goods.","about":["insurable-interest"],"facts":["insurable-interest.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-pledge","lv":"finance","d":5,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"What is meant by “Pledge”?","o":["security by a third party's promise to pay if the debtor doesn't","security by registering a charge over the goods in a public register","security by handing the goods, or their title documents, to the lender","security by keeping ownership of the goods until the whole price is paid"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Pledge: security by handing the goods, or their title documents, to the lender. The lender holds, the borrower still owns. A bank financing a cargo holds it in pledge through the bill of lading, and releasing the bill on a trust receipt so the buyer can sell keeps the pledge alive (North Western Bank v Poynter, 1895).","about":["pledge"],"facts":["pledge.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-pledge-name","lv":"finance","d":6,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"Which legal term means this? “Security by handing the goods, or their title documents, to the lender”","o":["Incoterms","Trust","Set-off","Pledge"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Pledge: security by handing the goods, or their title documents, to the lender. The lender holds, the borrower still owns. A bank financing a cargo holds it in pledge through the bill of lading, and releasing the bill on a trust receipt so the buyer can sell keeps the pledge alive (North Western Bank v Poynter, 1895).","about":["pledge"],"facts":["pledge.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-set-off","lv":"finance","d":5,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"What is meant by “Set-off”?","o":["a debt is cancelled once the debtor goes into insolvency","a debt is passed to a new creditor, who collects it in full","debts each side owes the other cancel out to leave the balance","a debt is put off to a later date by agreement between both sides"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Set-off: debts each side owes the other cancel out to leave the balance. Mandatory in an English insolvency (rule 14.25), where it lets a creditor recover in full up to what it owes. Trade contracts often exclude it ('without set-off or counterclaim'), so the price is paid first and the quarrel comes after.","about":["set-off"],"facts":["set-off.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-set-off-name","lv":"finance","d":6,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"Which legal term means this? “Debts each side owes the other cancel out to leave the balance”","o":["Subrogation","Pledge","Incoterms","Set-off"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Set-off: debts each side owes the other cancel out to leave the balance. Mandatory in an English insolvency (rule 14.25), where it lets a creditor recover in full up to what it owes. Trade contracts often exclude it ('without set-off or counterclaim'), so the price is paid first and the quarrel comes after.","about":["set-off"],"facts":["set-off.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-invitation-to-treat","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Invitation to treat”?","o":["an offer that binds as soon as anyone accepts it by acting on it","a step that only asks for offers, like goods on a shop shelf","an offer that must stay open for a set time once it has been made","an offer that can only be accepted in the way it lays down"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Invitation to treat: a step that only asks for offers, like goods on a shop shelf. Goods on a shelf (Pharmaceutical Society v Boots, 1953), adverts and price lists: the customer makes the offer and the seller accepts. An advert can be an offer if it's clear enough (Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball, 1893).","about":["invitation-to-treat"],"facts":["invitation-to-treat.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-invitation-to-treat-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “A step that only asks for offers, like goods on a shop shelf”","o":["Anticipatory breach","Misrepresentation","Invitation to treat","Economic duress"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Invitation to treat: a step that only asks for offers, like goods on a shop shelf. Goods on a shelf (Pharmaceutical Society v Boots, 1953), adverts and price lists: the customer makes the offer and the seller accepts. An advert can be an offer if it's clear enough (Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball, 1893).","about":["invitation-to-treat"],"facts":["invitation-to-treat.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-postal-rule","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Postal rule”?","o":["an offer sent by letter can't be withdrawn once it has been sent","a contract made by letter is governed by the law where it's received","an acceptance sent by letter binds when it's sent, not when it arrives","an acceptance sent by letter binds only when it arrives and is read"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Postal rule: an acceptance sent by letter binds when it's sent, not when it arrives. Adams v Lindsell (1818). It never applied to instant means: a telex or an email binds when it reaches the offeror (Entores v Miles Far East, 1955). The CISG takes the other view: an acceptance takes effect when it reaches the offeror (art. 18).","about":["postal-rule"],"facts":["postal-rule.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-postal-rule-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “An acceptance sent by letter binds when it's sent, not when it arrives”","o":["Mitigation","Consideration","Misrepresentation","Postal rule"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Postal rule: an acceptance sent by letter binds when it's sent, not when it arrives. Adams v Lindsell (1818). It never applied to instant means: a telex or an email binds when it reaches the offeror (Entores v Miles Far East, 1955). The CISG takes the other view: an acceptance takes effect when it reaches the offeror (art. 18).","about":["postal-rule"],"facts":["postal-rule.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-implied-terms","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Implied terms”?","o":["provisions the parties agreed by word of mouth but never wrote down","provisions a court strikes out because they're unfair to a consumer","provisions read into a deal because it couldn't work without them","provisions that bind only if the other side has signed beside them"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Implied terms: provisions read into a deal because it couldn't work without them. Implied in fact by The Moorcock's business efficacy (1889) or the officious bystander's 'oh, of course!' (Shirlaw v Southern Foundries, 1939), never merely because reasonable (Marks & Spencer v BNP Paribas, 2015). Others are implied by law, as by the Sale of Goods Act.","about":["implied-terms"],"facts":["implied-terms.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-implied-terms-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “Provisions read into a deal because it couldn't work without them”","o":["Implied terms","Consideration","Economic duress","Frustration"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Implied terms: provisions read into a deal because it couldn't work without them. Implied in fact by The Moorcock's business efficacy (1889) or the officious bystander's 'oh, of course!' (Shirlaw v Southern Foundries, 1939), never merely because reasonable (Marks & Spencer v BNP Paribas, 2015). Others are implied by law, as by the Sale of Goods Act.","about":["implied-terms"],"facts":["implied-terms.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-economic-duress","lv":"contract","d":7,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Economic duress”?","o":["illegitimate pressure on a business that left it no real choice","a sudden rise in costs that makes the contract ruinous to perform","hard bargaining by the stronger party over price, which courts allow","a threat of physical harm to a party, which makes its contract void"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Economic duress: illegitimate pressure on a business that left it no real choice. A threat to break a contract can be enough (The Atlantic Baron, 1979); a lawful act very rarely is (Pakistan International Airline v Times Travel, 2021). The contract is voidable, and the victim must act promptly.","about":["economic-duress"],"facts":["economic-duress.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-economic-duress-name","lv":"contract","d":8,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “Illegitimate pressure on a business that left it no real choice”","o":["Consideration","Invitation to treat","Anticipatory breach","Economic duress"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Economic duress: illegitimate pressure on a business that left it no real choice. A threat to break a contract can be enough (The Atlantic Baron, 1979); a lawful act very rarely is (Pakistan International Airline v Times Travel, 2021). The contract is voidable, and the victim must act promptly.","about":["economic-duress"],"facts":["economic-duress.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-conversion","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What is meant by “Conversion”?","o":["swapping a debt for shares in the company that owes it","dealing with goods in a way that denies the owner's right to them","taking goods openly and returning them before the owner asks","changing goods into a new product, which then belongs to its maker"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Conversion: dealing with goods in a way that denies the owner's right to them. Strict: honesty is no defence. A carrier or terminal that hands cargo to someone without the bill of lading converts it, against the holder; hence the letters of indemnity.","about":["conversion"],"facts":["conversion.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-conversion-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “Dealing with goods in a way that denies the owner's right to them”","o":["But-for test","Incoterms","Duty of care","Conversion"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Conversion: dealing with goods in a way that denies the owner's right to them. Strict: honesty is no defence. A carrier or terminal that hands cargo to someone without the bill of lading converts it, against the holder; hence the letters of indemnity.","about":["conversion"],"facts":["conversion.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-but-for-test","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What is meant by “But-for test”?","o":["harm is caused by a wrong if it would not have happened without it","harm is caused by a wrong if the wrong was its last cause in time","harm is caused by a wrong if the wrongdoer meant it to happen","harm is caused by a wrong if a reasonable person would say so"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"But-for test: harm is caused by a wrong if it would not have happened without it. Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington Hospital (1969): the doctor who sent a poisoned night-watchman home wasn't liable, as he'd have died anyway. The Romans' conditio sine qua non; relaxed for mesothelioma (Fairchild, 2002).","about":["but-for-test"],"facts":["but-for-test.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-but-for-test-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “Harm is caused by a wrong if it would not have happened without it”","o":["Penalty rule","Frustration","Contributory negligence","But-for test"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"But-for test: harm is caused by a wrong if it would not have happened without it. Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington Hospital (1969): the doctor who sent a poisoned night-watchman home wasn't liable, as he'd have died anyway. The Romans' conditio sine qua non; relaxed for mesothelioma (Fairchild, 2002).","about":["but-for-test"],"facts":["but-for-test.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-mfn","lv":"states","d":5,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"What is meant by “Most-favoured-nation treatment”?","o":["imported goods must fare no worse than local ones once inside","a member may raise duties on a country that dumps goods on it","a member must give its best tariff to its oldest trading partners","a trade advantage given to one member must go to all at once"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Most-favoured-nation treatment: a trade advantage given to one member must go to all at once. GATT 1947, art. I, the first rule of the WTO: no discrimination between trading partners. Exceptions: customs unions and free trade areas (art. XXIV) and preferences for developing countries. The US revoked Russia's in 2022.","about":["mfn"],"facts":["mfn.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-mfn-name","lv":"states","d":6,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"Which legal term means this? “A trade advantage given to one member must go to all at once”","o":["Specific performance","National treatment","Anticipatory breach","Most-favoured-nation treatment"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Most-favoured-nation treatment: a trade advantage given to one member must go to all at once. GATT 1947, art. I, the first rule of the WTO: no discrimination between trading partners. Exceptions: customs unions and free trade areas (art. XXIV) and preferences for developing countries. The US revoked Russia's in 2022.","about":["mfn"],"facts":["mfn.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-national-treatment","lv":"states","d":5,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"What is meant by “National treatment”?","o":["once imported, goods must carry a label giving their country of origin","once imported, goods may be taxed only at the rate bound at the WTO","once imported, goods may be sold only by firms owned by locals","once imported, goods must fare no worse than local goods"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"National treatment: once imported, goods must fare no worse than local goods. GATT, art. III. Carbon border taxes and sustainability rules for biofuels must meet it, or be justified under the general exceptions of article XX.","about":["national-treatment"],"facts":["national-treatment.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-national-treatment-name","lv":"states","d":6,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"Which legal term means this? “Once imported, goods must fare no worse than local goods”","o":["Most-favoured-nation treatment","Insurable interest","National treatment","Bona fide purchaser"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"National treatment: once imported, goods must fare no worse than local goods. GATT, art. III. Carbon border taxes and sustainability rules for biofuels must meet it, or be justified under the general exceptions of article XX.","about":["national-treatment"],"facts":["national-treatment.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-consideration","lv":"contract","d":3,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Consideration”?","o":["the courtesy each party owes the other while the contract runs","a party's right to think an offer over before it must answer","something of value each side gives for the other's promise","a court's duty to weigh each side's case fairly before ruling"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Consideration: something of value each side gives for the other's promise. What makes a promise a bargain in the common law: it must move from the promisee, needn't be adequate (a peppercorn will do), and past consideration is no consideration. Without it a promise binds only by deed; civil law needs no such thing.","about":["consideration"],"facts":["consideration.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-consideration-name","lv":"contract","d":4,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “Something of value each side gives for the other's promise”","o":["Consideration","Penalty rule","Postal rule","Economic duress"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Consideration: something of value each side gives for the other's promise. What makes a promise a bargain in the common law: it must move from the promisee, needn't be adequate (a peppercorn will do), and past consideration is no consideration. Without it a promise binds only by deed; civil law needs no such thing.","about":["consideration"],"facts":["consideration.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-privity","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What is meant by “Privity of contract”?","o":["a contract binds whoever later buys the goods it concerns","a contract made in private needn't be registered to bind","only the parties to an agreement can enforce it or be bound by it","a contract's terms must be kept secret from anyone outside the deal"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Privity of contract: only the parties to an agreement can enforce it or be bound by it. Dunlop v Selfridge (1915). Softened by the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999: a third party may enforce a term that names it or is meant to benefit it, unless the contract says otherwise, as most commercial contracts do.","about":["privity"],"facts":["privity.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-privity-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “Only the parties to an agreement can enforce it or be bound by it”","o":["Consideration","Specific performance","Invitation to treat","Privity of contract"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Privity of contract: only the parties to an agreement can enforce it or be bound by it. Dunlop v Selfridge (1915). Softened by the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999: a third party may enforce a term that names it or is meant to benefit it, unless the contract says otherwise, as most commercial contracts do.","about":["privity"],"facts":["privity.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-caveat-emptor-name","lv":"sale","d":4,"area":"Selling goods","q":"Which legal term means this? “The buyer is responsible for checking what they buy”","o":["Nemo dat quod non habet","Lex talionis","Habeas corpus","Caveat emptor"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Caveat emptor: the buyer is responsible for checking what they buy. Literally: let the buyer beware. The common law's old rule for sales, now much narrowed: the Sale of Goods Act 1979 implies that goods sold in the course of a business are of satisfactory quality and fit for their purpose (s 14). It still holds for private sales and, largely, for land.","about":["caveat-emptor"],"facts":["caveat-emptor.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-quid-pro-quo-name","lv":"contract","d":4,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “Something given in return for something else”","o":["Bona fide","Locus standi","Contra proferentem","Quid pro quo"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Quid pro quo: something given in return for something else. Literally: something for something. The bargain at the heart of a contract: each side gives something for what it gets. In the common law that is consideration; in a bribery case, a quid pro quo is what makes a favour a corrupt exchange.","about":["quid-pro-quo"],"facts":["quid-pro-quo.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ad-hoc-name","lv":"disputes","d":4,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “Made for one particular purpose, not planned”","o":["Pro bono","Ad hoc","Res judicata","Ratio decidendi"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Ad hoc: made for one particular purpose, not planned. Literally: for this. An ad hoc arbitration is one with no institution to run it: the parties and their arbitrators set the rules, usually the UNCITRAL Rules, and the court of the seat helps when they cannot agree.","about":["ad-hoc"],"facts":["ad-hoc.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-pro-bono-name","lv":"disputes","d":4,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “Done for free, for the public good”","o":["Res judicata","Ratio decidendi","Locus standi","Pro bono"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Pro bono: done for free, for the public good. Literally: for the good. From pro bono publico, for the public good: legal work done without a fee, which many lawyers' professional rules encourage.","about":["pro-bono"],"facts":["pro-bono.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-bona-fide-name","lv":"contract","d":4,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “Genuine, in good faith”","o":["Pro forma","Quantum meruit","Rebus sic stantibus","Bona fide"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Bona fide: genuine, in good faith. Literally: in good faith. In good faith: honest, and without notice of any defect or adverse claim. Several exceptions to nemo dat turn on it, and so does a holder in due course's title.","about":["bona-fide"],"facts":["bona-fide.meaning","bona-fide.literal"]},
{"id":"LW-habeas-corpus-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “The right not to be held without a court's review”","o":["Habeas corpus","Sine qua non","Respondeat superior","Ne bis in idem"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Habeas corpus: the right not to be held without a court's review. Literally: you shall have the body. The writ by which a court orders a prisoner brought before it to test whether the detention is lawful; the Habeas Corpus Act 1679 made it effective, and Magna Carta's clause 39 is its root.","about":["habeas-corpus"],"facts":["habeas-corpus.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-de-facto-name","lv":"states","d":4,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"Which legal term means this? “In fact, whether or not by right”","o":["Mare liberum","Bona fide","De facto","Pro forma"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"De facto: in fact, whether or not by right. Literally: from the fact. Opposite of de jure, by right. A de facto government holds the territory and the power without being recognised as the lawful one; a de facto director acts as one without having been appointed.","about":["de-facto"],"facts":["de-facto.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-sine-qua-non-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “An essential condition”","o":["In dubio pro reo","Ne bis in idem","Res ipsa loquitur","Sine qua non"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Sine qua non: an essential condition. Literally: without which not. The Roman lawyers' conditio sine qua non is the but-for test of causation: take away the act and the harm would not have happened. Necessary, but not enough, because it isn't a test of fault or foresight.","about":["sine-qua-non"],"facts":["sine-qua-non.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-mutatis-mutandis-name","lv":"reading","d":6,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “With the necessary changes made”","o":["Ejusdem generis","Ipso facto","Noscitur a sociis","Mutatis mutandis"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Mutatis mutandis: with the necessary changes made. Literally: having changed what must be changed. A drafting shortcut: 'section 5 applies, mutatis mutandis, to a lease' extends a rule to a new case with whatever changes of names and details make it fit.","about":["mutatis-mutandis"],"facts":["mutatis-mutandis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-pro-forma-name","lv":"sale","d":6,"area":"Selling goods","q":"Which legal term means this? “As a matter of form”","o":["Pro forma","Caveat emptor","Nemo dat quod non habet","Ipso facto"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Pro forma: as a matter of form. Literally: for form. A pro forma invoice is a quotation set out like an invoice, not a demand for payment; a buyer needs it to open a credit or to get an import licence. It is not a tax invoice.","about":["pro-forma"],"facts":["pro-forma.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ipso-facto-name","lv":"reading","d":6,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “By that very fact”","o":["Ipso facto","Per incuriam","Ejusdem generis","Noscitur a sociis"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Ipso facto: by that very fact. An 'ipso facto' clause ends a contract automatically on the other side's insolvency. English and US law now restrict them: the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020 stops a supplier from terminating just because its customer enters a formal procedure.","about":["ipso-facto"],"facts":["ipso-facto.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-force-majeure-name","lv":"contract","d":4,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “An unforeseeable event that excuses a party from a contract”","o":["Pacta sunt servanda","Contra proferentem","Habeas corpus","Force majeure"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Force majeure: an unforeseeable event that excuses a party from a contract. Literally: superior force. Not a doctrine of English law but a clause, read for what it says: which events, whether they must prevent or merely hinder, what notice. A party needn't accept performance the contract doesn't provide for to overcome one (RTI v MUR Shipping, 2024: euros offered for sanctioned dollars).","about":["force-majeure"],"facts":["force-majeure.meaning","force-majeure.branch"]},
{"id":"LW-pari-passu","lv":"finance","d":5,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"What does this mean? “Pari passu” (literally: with equal step)","o":["a new partner joins on equal terms with all the founding partners","both parties must perform at the same moment, neither first","a loan is repaid in equal steps, the same sum every month","creditors of the same rank share in proportion to their claims"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Pari passu: creditors of the same rank share in proportion to their claims. Literally: with equal step. The default rule for unsecured creditors in an insolvency, and a standard clause in loans and bonds. Read as a duty to pay all holders ratably, it let Elliott freeze Argentina's payments on its restructured bonds from 2012 until 2016.","about":["pari-passu"],"facts":["pari-passu.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-pari-passu-name","lv":"finance","d":6,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"Which legal term means this? “Creditors of the same rank share in proportion to their claims”","o":["Lis pendens","Erga omnes","Uberrimae fidei","Pari passu"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Pari passu: creditors of the same rank share in proportion to their claims. Literally: with equal step. The default rule for unsecured creditors in an insolvency, and a standard clause in loans and bonds. Read as a duty to pay all holders ratably, it let Elliott freeze Argentina's payments on its restructured bonds from 2012 until 2016.","about":["pari-passu"],"facts":["pari-passu.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-pacta-sunt-servanda","lv":"contract","d":3,"area":"Contracts","q":"What does this mean? “Pacta sunt servanda”","o":["agreements bind only their parties, never anyone else","agreements made under pressure can be set aside by a court","agreements bind, and must be carried out in good faith","agreements bind only once they're written down and signed"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Pacta sunt servanda: agreements bind, and must be carried out in good faith. Literally: agreements must be kept. The first rule of contract in the civil law, and of treaties in international law (Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, art. 26). Its counterweight is rebus sic stantibus.","about":["pacta-sunt-servanda"],"facts":["pacta-sunt-servanda.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-pacta-sunt-servanda-name","lv":"contract","d":4,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “Agreements bind, and must be carried out in good faith”","o":["Quantum meruit","Audi alteram partem","Pacta sunt servanda","Respondeat superior"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Pacta sunt servanda: agreements bind, and must be carried out in good faith. Literally: agreements must be kept. The first rule of contract in the civil law, and of treaties in international law (Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, art. 26). Its counterweight is rebus sic stantibus.","about":["pacta-sunt-servanda"],"facts":["pacta-sunt-servanda.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-res-judicata","lv":"disputes","d":3,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What does this mean? “Res judicata”","o":["a matter still before one court can't be brought before another","a matter finally decided can't be fought again by the same parties","a claim can't be brought once the time allowed for bringing it has run out","a court must follow the earlier decisions of the courts above it"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Res judicata: a matter finally decided can't be fought again by the same parties. Literally: a thing judged. With issue estoppel and the rule in Henderson v Henderson (1843), which bars points that should have been raised the first time. An arbitral award is res judicata between its parties too.","about":["res-judicata"],"facts":["res-judicata.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-res-judicata-name","lv":"disputes","d":4,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “A matter finally decided can't be fought again by the same parties”","o":["Stare decisis","Pro bono","Locus standi","Res judicata"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Res judicata: a matter finally decided can't be fought again by the same parties. Literally: a thing judged. With issue estoppel and the rule in Henderson v Henderson (1843), which bars points that should have been raised the first time. An arbitral award is res judicata between its parties too.","about":["res-judicata"],"facts":["res-judicata.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-stare-decisis","lv":"disputes","d":3,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What does this mean? “Stare decisis”","o":["a judge must decide the case on the evidence given, nothing else","a matter finally decided can't be fought again between the same parties","courts follow the earlier rulings of higher courts on the same point","a judgment stays in force until an appeal court sets it aside"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Stare decisis: courts follow the earlier rulings of higher courts on the same point. Literally: to stand by things decided. Only the ratio decidendi binds, not obiter dicta. The UK Supreme Court may depart from its own decisions (Practice Statement, 1966); civil law courts follow settled case law without a formal rule.","about":["stare-decisis"],"facts":["stare-decisis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-stare-decisis-name","lv":"disputes","d":4,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “Courts follow the earlier rulings of higher courts on the same point”","o":["Obiter dictum","Audi alteram partem","Res judicata","Stare decisis"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Stare decisis: courts follow the earlier rulings of higher courts on the same point. Literally: to stand by things decided. Only the ratio decidendi binds, not obiter dicta. The UK Supreme Court may depart from its own decisions (Practice Statement, 1966); civil law courts follow settled case law without a formal rule.","about":["stare-decisis"],"facts":["stare-decisis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-obiter-dictum","lv":"disputes","d":5,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What does this mean? “Obiter dictum” (literally: a thing said by the way)","o":["a judgment given without reasons, as some courts may do","a remark in a judgment not needed for the result, so not binding","a ruling given before the full hearing, to keep things as they are","a judge's note to the parties, sent after the judgment is given"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Obiter dictum: a remark in a judgment not needed for the result, so not binding. Literally: a thing said by the way. Not binding, but a considered obiter from a high court is often followed: Hedley Byrne v Heller (1964), which gave English law liability for negligent misstatement, was strictly obiter.","about":["obiter-dictum"],"facts":["obiter-dictum.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-obiter-dictum-name","lv":"disputes","d":6,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “A remark in a judgment not needed for the result, so not binding”","o":["Locus standi","Lis pendens","Obiter dictum","Pro bono"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Obiter dictum: a remark in a judgment not needed for the result, so not binding. Literally: a thing said by the way. Not binding, but a considered obiter from a high court is often followed: Hedley Byrne v Heller (1964), which gave English law liability for negligent misstatement, was strictly obiter.","about":["obiter-dictum"],"facts":["obiter-dictum.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-locus-standi","lv":"disputes","d":5,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What does this mean? “Locus standi” (literally: a place to stand)","o":["the place where the thing in dispute lies, whose law governs it","the right to bring a claim, by having a sufficient interest in it","the right of a party to be present in person at its own hearing","the court where a claim must be brought, by where the defendant lives"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Locus standi: the right to bring a claim, by having a sufficient interest in it. Literally: a place to stand. In judicial review, a 'sufficient interest in the matter'. Before the EU's courts, a private party may challenge an act only if it concerns it directly and individually (Plaumann, 1963).","about":["locus-standi"],"facts":["locus-standi.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-locus-standi-name","lv":"disputes","d":6,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “The right to bring a claim, by having a sufficient interest in it”","o":["Stare decisis","Locus standi","Ratio decidendi","Res judicata"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Locus standi: the right to bring a claim, by having a sufficient interest in it. Literally: a place to stand. In judicial review, a 'sufficient interest in the matter'. Before the EU's courts, a private party may challenge an act only if it concerns it directly and individually (Plaumann, 1963).","about":["locus-standi"],"facts":["locus-standi.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-lis-pendens","lv":"disputes","d":7,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What does this mean? “Lis pendens” (literally: a suit pending)","o":["a claim not yet decided may be dropped without paying the costs","a claim may be paused while the parties try to settle it","a dispute already before one court can't be begun in another","a claim can't be brought until an earlier appeal is decided"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Lis pendens: a dispute already before one court can't be begun in another. Literally: a suit pending. Brussels I (recast), art. 29: the court second seised waits until the first decides whether it has jurisdiction, though a court chosen in an exclusive jurisdiction clause now goes first (art. 31(2)), ending the 'Italian torpedo'.","about":["lis-pendens"],"facts":["lis-pendens.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-lis-pendens-name","lv":"disputes","d":8,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “A dispute already before one court can't be begun in another”","o":["Ad hoc","Obiter dictum","Ratio decidendi","Lis pendens"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Lis pendens: a dispute already before one court can't be begun in another. Literally: a suit pending. Brussels I (recast), art. 29: the court second seised waits until the first decides whether it has jurisdiction, though a court chosen in an exclusive jurisdiction clause now goes first (art. 31(2)), ending the 'Italian torpedo'.","about":["lis-pendens"],"facts":["lis-pendens.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-de-minimis","lv":"reading","d":3,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “De minimis non curat lex”","o":["the law ignores matters too small to be worth its time","the law punishes small crimes as firmly as great ones","the law protects the weakest party first in any dispute","the law treats acts of minors more leniently than adults'"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"De minimis non curat lex: the law ignores matters too small to be worth its time. Literally: the law doesn't concern itself with trifles. In trade too: a buyer couldn't reject a wheat cargo for being a trifle over its contract quantity (Shipton, Anderson v Weil Brothers, 1912). WTO law has de minimis thresholds: no anti-dumping duty on a margin under 2%.","about":["de-minimis"],"facts":["de-minimis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-de-minimis-name","lv":"reading","d":4,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “The law ignores matters too small to be worth its time”","o":["De minimis non curat lex","Mutatis mutandis","Dura lex, sed lex","Ut res magis valeat quam pereat"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"De minimis non curat lex: the law ignores matters too small to be worth its time. Literally: the law doesn't concern itself with trifles. In trade too: a buyer couldn't reject a wheat cargo for being a trifle over its contract quantity (Shipton, Anderson v Weil Brothers, 1912). WTO law has de minimis thresholds: no anti-dumping duty on a margin under 2%.","about":["de-minimis"],"facts":["de-minimis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ne-bis-in-idem","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What does this mean? “Ne bis in idem”","o":["no one may be tried or punished twice for the same offence","no one may be sued in two countries' courts at the same time","no one may be made to pay the same debt to two creditors","no one may be tried by a judge who has heard their case before"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Ne bis in idem: no one may be tried or punished twice for the same offence. Literally: not twice for the same. Double jeopardy: in the European Convention (Protocol 7, art. 4), the EU Charter (art. 50) and the German Basic Law (art. 103(3)). England allows a retrial for murder and other grave crimes on compelling new evidence (Criminal Justice Act 2003).","about":["ne-bis-in-idem"],"facts":["ne-bis-in-idem.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ne-bis-in-idem-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “No one may be tried or punished twice for the same offence”","o":["Lex talionis","Ne bis in idem","Res ipsa loquitur","In dubio pro reo"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Ne bis in idem: no one may be tried or punished twice for the same offence. Literally: not twice for the same. Double jeopardy: in the European Convention (Protocol 7, art. 4), the EU Charter (art. 50) and the German Basic Law (art. 103(3)). England allows a retrial for murder and other grave crimes on compelling new evidence (Criminal Justice Act 2003).","about":["ne-bis-in-idem"],"facts":["ne-bis-in-idem.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-in-dubio-pro-reo","lv":"wrongs","d":3,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What does this mean? “In dubio pro reo”","o":["a doubt about a contract's words is resolved against its writer","a doubt about a witness is resolved by hearing them a second time","a doubt about the law must be resolved in the state's favour","a doubt about the facts must be resolved in the accused's favour"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"In dubio pro reo: a doubt about the facts must be resolved in the accused's favour. Literally: in doubt, for the accused. Behind the presumption of innocence and the criminal standard, proof beyond reasonable doubt. It governs the facts, not how the law is read.","about":["in-dubio-pro-reo"],"facts":["in-dubio-pro-reo.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-in-dubio-pro-reo-name","lv":"wrongs","d":4,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “A doubt about the facts must be resolved in the accused's favour”","o":["Nullum crimen sine lege","Lex talionis","Habeas corpus","In dubio pro reo"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"In dubio pro reo: a doubt about the facts must be resolved in the accused's favour. Literally: in doubt, for the accused. Behind the presumption of innocence and the criminal standard, proof beyond reasonable doubt. It governs the facts, not how the law is read.","about":["in-dubio-pro-reo"],"facts":["in-dubio-pro-reo.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-audi-alteram-partem","lv":"disputes","d":3,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What does this mean? “Audi alteram partem”","o":["no one may be condemned without a chance to answer the case","no one may be made to give evidence against themselves","no one may be tried a second time for the same offence","no one may be judged by someone with a personal stake in the outcome"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Audi alteram partem: no one may be condemned without a chance to answer the case. Literally: hear the other side. With nemo iudex in causa sua, one of the two pillars of natural justice. An arbitral award made without it may be refused enforcement under the New York Convention (art. V(1)(b)).","about":["audi-alteram-partem"],"facts":["audi-alteram-partem.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-audi-alteram-partem-name","lv":"disputes","d":4,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “No one may be condemned without a chance to answer the case”","o":["Obiter dictum","Audi alteram partem","Nemo iudex in causa sua","Res judicata"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Audi alteram partem: no one may be condemned without a chance to answer the case. Literally: hear the other side. With nemo iudex in causa sua, one of the two pillars of natural justice. An arbitral award made without it may be refused enforcement under the New York Convention (art. V(1)(b)).","about":["audi-alteram-partem"],"facts":["audi-alteram-partem.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-nemo-iudex","lv":"disputes","d":3,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What does this mean? “Nemo iudex in causa sua”","o":["no one may decide a case in which they have an interest","no one may bring a claim on behalf of somebody else","no one may appeal a decision they agreed to in advance","no one may be condemned without a chance to answer the case"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Nemo iudex in causa sua: no one may decide a case in which they have an interest. Literally: no one is judge in their own cause. The appearance of bias is enough: the House of Lords set aside its own decision in Pinochet (1999) because Lord Hoffmann was linked to Amnesty International, a party. Arbitrators must disclose such links (Halliburton v Chubb, 2020).","about":["nemo-iudex"],"facts":["nemo-iudex.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-nemo-iudex-name","lv":"disputes","d":4,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “No one may decide a case in which they have an interest”","o":["Audi alteram partem","Obiter dictum","Res judicata","Nemo iudex in causa sua"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Nemo iudex in causa sua: no one may decide a case in which they have an interest. Literally: no one is judge in their own cause. The appearance of bias is enough: the House of Lords set aside its own decision in Pinochet (1999) because Lord Hoffmann was linked to Amnesty International, a party. Arbitrators must disclose such links (Halliburton v Chubb, 2020).","about":["nemo-iudex"],"facts":["nemo-iudex.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-rebus-sic-stantibus","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What does this mean? “Rebus sic stantibus” (literally: things standing thus)","o":["a court leaves the parties where it finds them, ordering nothing","the existing state of affairs must be kept until a dispute is settled","a fundamental change of circumstances can end or alter an obligation","a contract is read in the light of the facts known when it was made"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Rebus sic stantibus: a fundamental change of circumstances can end or alter an obligation. Literally: things standing thus. Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, art. 62, read narrowly. In German contract law, the Störung der Geschäftsgrundlage of § 313 BGB lets a court adjust a contract; English law has only the far stricter frustration.","about":["rebus-sic-stantibus"],"facts":["rebus-sic-stantibus.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-rebus-sic-stantibus-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “A fundamental change of circumstances can end or alter an obligation”","o":["Contra proferentem","Bona fide","Respondeat superior","Rebus sic stantibus"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Rebus sic stantibus: a fundamental change of circumstances can end or alter an obligation. Literally: things standing thus. Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, art. 62, read narrowly. In German contract law, the Störung der Geschäftsgrundlage of § 313 BGB lets a court adjust a contract; English law has only the far stricter frustration.","about":["rebus-sic-stantibus"],"facts":["rebus-sic-stantibus.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ultra-vires","lv":"companies","d":3,"area":"Companies and agents","q":"What does this mean? “Ultra vires”","o":["an act done abroad, beyond the reach of the home courts","an act done in bad faith by a body's officer, and so voidable","an act beyond the powers a body has, and so without effect","an act a body may do only with a court's prior approval"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Ultra vires: an act beyond the powers a body has, and so without effect. Literally: beyond the powers. Once fatal to companies' contracts; now a company's capacity can't be questioned because of its constitution (Companies Act 2006, s 39). It lives on in public law: a council's interest rate swaps were void (Hazell v Hammersmith and Fulham, 1991).","about":["ultra-vires"],"facts":["ultra-vires.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ultra-vires-name","lv":"companies","d":4,"area":"Companies and agents","q":"Which legal term means this? “An act beyond the powers a body has, and so without effect”","o":["Ipso facto","Res nullius","Locus standi","Ultra vires"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Ultra vires: an act beyond the powers a body has, and so without effect. Literally: beyond the powers. Once fatal to companies' contracts; now a company's capacity can't be questioned because of its constitution (Companies Act 2006, s 39). It lives on in public law: a council's interest rate swaps were void (Hazell v Hammersmith and Fulham, 1991).","about":["ultra-vires"],"facts":["ultra-vires.literal","ultra-vires.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-jus-cogens","lv":"states","d":7,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"What does this mean? “Jus cogens” (literally: compelling law)","o":["rules a court applies of its own motion, though not pleaded","rules binding only once the UN Security Council adopts them","rules no state may contract out of, like the ban on genocide","rules a state must enforce on every ship that sails under its flag"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Jus cogens: rules no state may contract out of, like the ban on genocide. Literally: compelling law. Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, art. 53: a treaty conflicting with a peremptory norm is void. Agreed examples: the bans on aggression, genocide, slavery, torture and racial discrimination.","about":["jus-cogens"],"facts":["jus-cogens.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-jus-cogens-name","lv":"states","d":8,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"Which legal term means this? “Rules no state may contract out of, like the ban on genocide”","o":["De facto","Ipso facto","Pari passu","Jus cogens"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Jus cogens: rules no state may contract out of, like the ban on genocide. Literally: compelling law. Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, art. 53: a treaty conflicting with a peremptory norm is void. Agreed examples: the bans on aggression, genocide, slavery, torture and racial discrimination.","about":["jus-cogens"],"facts":["jus-cogens.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-erga-omnes","lv":"states","d":7,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"What does this mean? “Erga omnes” (literally: towards all)","o":["a right a state holds against all of its citizens alike","owed to all states, so that any of them may invoke a breach","an offer open to all comers, accepted by whoever acts on it","a tax levied on all goods alike, whatever their origin"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Erga omnes: owed to all states, so that any of them may invoke a breach. Literally: towards all. Barcelona Traction (ICJ, 1970): obligations towards the international community as a whole, such as the bans on aggression and genocide. In property law, a right in rem holds erga omnes, a contract right only between the parties.","about":["erga-omnes"],"facts":["erga-omnes.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-erga-omnes-name","lv":"states","d":8,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"Which legal term means this? “Owed to all states, so that any of them may invoke a breach”","o":["Mare liberum","Bona fide","Jus cogens","Erga omnes"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Erga omnes: owed to all states, so that any of them may invoke a breach. Literally: towards all. Barcelona Traction (ICJ, 1970): obligations towards the international community as a whole, such as the bans on aggression and genocide. In property law, a right in rem holds erga omnes, a contract right only between the parties.","about":["erga-omnes"],"facts":["erga-omnes.branch","erga-omnes.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-mare-liberum","lv":"states","d":5,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"What does this mean? “Mare liberum” (literally: the free sea)","o":["the high seas belong to no state and are open to every ship for trade","a ship may enter any port free of duties if it carries no cargo","a coastal state may tax ships passing within sight of its shores","a ship at sea answers to no law but its own master's command"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Mare liberum: the high seas belong to no state and are open to every ship for trade. Literally: the free sea. Grotius (1609), written for the Dutch East India Company against Portugal's claim to the Indian Ocean and answered by Selden's Mare clausum (1635). UNCLOS (1982) splits the difference: 12 miles of territorial sea, a 200-mile economic zone, the high seas beyond.","about":["mare-liberum"],"facts":["mare-liberum.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-mare-liberum-name","lv":"states","d":6,"area":"States and trade rules","q":"Which legal term means this? “The high seas belong to no state and are open to every ship for trade”","o":["De facto","Locus standi","Mare liberum","Per incuriam"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Mare liberum: the high seas belong to no state and are open to every ship for trade. Literally: the free sea. Grotius (1609), written for the Dutch East India Company against Portugal's claim to the Indian Ocean and answered by Selden's Mare clausum (1635). UNCLOS (1982) splits the difference: 12 miles of territorial sea, a 200-mile economic zone, the high seas beyond.","about":["mare-liberum"],"facts":["mare-liberum.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ex-turpi-causa","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What does this mean? “Ex turpi causa non oritur actio” (literally: no action arises from a base cause)","o":["a claim brought from an improper motive must be struck out","a claimant can't build a claim on its own illegal act","a defendant who acted from a base motive pays extra damages","a contract made at a bad price can't be enforced by the gainer"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Ex turpi causa non oritur actio: a claimant can't build a claim on its own illegal act. Literally: no action arises from a base cause. Since Patel v Mirza (2016) not applied mechanically: a court asks whether enforcing the claim would harm the integrity of the law, weighing the purpose of the rule broken, other policies and proportion.","about":["ex-turpi-causa"],"facts":["ex-turpi-causa.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ex-turpi-causa-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “A claimant can't build a claim on its own illegal act”","o":["In dubio pro reo","Nullum crimen sine lege","Nemo tenetur se ipsum accusare","Ex turpi causa non oritur actio"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Ex turpi causa non oritur actio: a claimant can't build a claim on its own illegal act. Literally: no action arises from a base cause. Since Patel v Mirza (2016) not applied mechanically: a court asks whether enforcing the claim would harm the integrity of the law, weighing the purpose of the rule broken, other policies and proportion.","about":["ex-turpi-causa"],"facts":["ex-turpi-causa.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-res-ipsa-loquitur","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What does this mean? “Res ipsa loquitur” (literally: the thing speaks for itself)","o":["an accident that wouldn't happen without negligence implies it","a thing found in someone's hands is presumed to be theirs","a document's meaning is taken from its own words, nothing else","the facts are so plain that the court needs to hear no argument on them"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Res ipsa loquitur: an accident that wouldn't happen without negligence implies it. Literally: the thing speaks for itself. Byrne v Boadle (1863): a barrel of flour fell from a warehouse window onto a passer-by. It puts the burden of explaining on the defendant.","about":["res-ipsa-loquitur"],"facts":["res-ipsa-loquitur.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-res-ipsa-loquitur-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “An accident that wouldn't happen without negligence implies it”","o":["Lex talionis","Ne bis in idem","Habeas corpus","Res ipsa loquitur"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Res ipsa loquitur: an accident that wouldn't happen without negligence implies it. Literally: the thing speaks for itself. Byrne v Boadle (1863): a barrel of flour fell from a warehouse window onto a passer-by. It puts the burden of explaining on the defendant.","about":["res-ipsa-loquitur"],"facts":["res-ipsa-loquitur.meaning","res-ipsa-loquitur>link>duty-of-care"]},
{"id":"LW-volenti","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What does this mean? “Volenti non fit injuria” (literally: to one who is willing, no wrong is done)","o":["a wrong done willingly is punished more than one by accident","someone who agreed to a contract can't later call it unfair","someone who helps in an emergency can't be sued for the harm","someone who freely accepts a risk can't sue for the harm"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Volenti non fit injuria: someone who freely accepts a risk can't sue for the harm. Literally: to one who is willing, no wrong is done. Narrow: knowing of a risk isn't agreeing to it, and a rescuer is never volens (Haynes v Harwood, 1935). It can't be pleaded against employees, or against passengers in cars.","about":["volenti"],"facts":["volenti.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-volenti-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “Someone who freely accepts a risk can't sue for the harm”","o":["Ne bis in idem","Nullum crimen sine lege","Nemo tenetur se ipsum accusare","Volenti non fit injuria"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Volenti non fit injuria: someone who freely accepts a risk can't sue for the harm. Literally: to one who is willing, no wrong is done. Narrow: knowing of a risk isn't agreeing to it, and a rescuer is never volens (Haynes v Harwood, 1935). It can't be pleaded against employees, or against passengers in cars.","about":["volenti"],"facts":["volenti.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-respondeat-superior","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What does this mean? “Respondeat superior” (literally: let the superior answer)","o":["an appeal court must answer a question sent up by a lower court","a senior officer answers for a junior's crime as if it were his own","an employer is liable for wrongs its employees commit at work","a parent company answers for every debt of its subsidiaries"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Respondeat superior: an employer is liable for wrongs its employees commit at work. Literally: let the superior answer. Vicarious liability: strict, but only for acts closely connected with the job (Lister v Hesley Hall, 2001), and not for an independent contractor's (Various Claimants v Barclays, 2020).","about":["respondeat-superior"],"facts":["respondeat-superior.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-respondeat-superior-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “An employer is liable for wrongs its employees commit at work”","o":["Respondeat superior","Volenti non fit injuria","In dubio pro reo","Habeas corpus"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Respondeat superior: an employer is liable for wrongs its employees commit at work. Literally: let the superior answer. Vicarious liability: strict, but only for acts closely connected with the job (Lister v Hesley Hall, 2001), and not for an independent contractor's (Various Claimants v Barclays, 2020).","about":["respondeat-superior"],"facts":["respondeat-superior.branch","respondeat-superior.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-actus-non-facit-reum","lv":"wrongs","d":3,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What does this mean? “Actus non facit reum nisi mens sit rea”","o":["a crime needs a victim who complains as well as an act","a crime needs a guilty mind as well as a guilty act","a crime planned but never carried out can't be punished","a crime done under orders is the commander's, not the soldier's"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Actus non facit reum nisi mens sit rea: a crime needs a guilty mind as well as a guilty act. Literally: the act doesn't make one guilty unless the mind is guilty. Coke's maxim, behind a crime's two elements: actus reus, the guilty act, and mens rea, the guilty mind (intention, recklessness, knowledge). Strict liability offences, common in regulation, need no mens rea.","about":["actus-non-facit-reum"],"facts":["actus-non-facit-reum.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-actus-non-facit-reum-name","lv":"wrongs","d":4,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “A crime needs a guilty mind as well as a guilty act”","o":["Ex turpi causa non oritur actio","Actus non facit reum nisi mens sit rea","Respondeat superior","Nemo tenetur se ipsum accusare"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Actus non facit reum nisi mens sit rea: a crime needs a guilty mind as well as a guilty act. Literally: the act doesn't make one guilty unless the mind is guilty. Coke's maxim, behind a crime's two elements: actus reus, the guilty act, and mens rea, the guilty mind (intention, recklessness, knowledge). Strict liability offences, common in regulation, need no mens rea.","about":["actus-non-facit-reum"],"facts":["actus-non-facit-reum.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-nullum-crimen","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What does this mean? “Nullum crimen sine lege” (literally: no crime without a law)","o":["no one can be punished for what the law hadn't forbidden when done","no one can be punished for a crime the police never saw committed","no one can be punished twice under two laws for one act","no one can be punished at home for a crime done abroad"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Nullum crimen sine lege: no one can be punished for what the law hadn't forbidden when done. Literally: no crime without a law. With nulla poena sine lege, in Feuerbach's form (1801): the European Convention on Human Rights, art. 7, and the German Basic Law, art. 103(2). Retroactive criminal laws are barred.","about":["nullum-crimen"],"facts":["nullum-crimen.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-nullum-crimen-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “No one can be punished for what the law hadn't forbidden when done”","o":["Nemo tenetur se ipsum accusare","Volenti non fit injuria","Respondeat superior","Nullum crimen sine lege"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Nullum crimen sine lege: no one can be punished for what the law hadn't forbidden when done. Literally: no crime without a law. With nulla poena sine lege, in Feuerbach's form (1801): the European Convention on Human Rights, art. 7, and the German Basic Law, art. 103(2). Retroactive criminal laws are barred.","about":["nullum-crimen"],"facts":["nullum-crimen.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-lex-talionis","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What does this mean? “Lex talionis” (literally: the law of retaliation)","o":["a punishment equal to the harm done, and never more than that","a state may strike back at once and in kind when it's attacked","a victim's family may choose the punishment of the guilty","a creditor may seize the debtor's goods himself, without a court"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Lex talionis: a punishment equal to the harm done, and never more than that. Literally: the law of retaliation. Hammurabi's laws (about 1754 BC), the Torah's 'an eye for an eye' and Rome's Twelve Tables. Its point was a limit on revenge as much as a measure of it.","about":["lex-talionis"],"facts":["lex-talionis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-lex-talionis-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “A punishment equal to the harm done, and never more than that”","o":["Nullum crimen sine lege","Respondeat superior","In dubio pro reo","Lex talionis"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Lex talionis: a punishment equal to the harm done, and never more than that. Literally: the law of retaliation. Hammurabi's laws (about 1754 BC), the Torah's 'an eye for an eye' and Rome's Twelve Tables. Its point was a limit on revenge as much as a measure of it.","about":["lex-talionis"],"facts":["lex-talionis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ignorantia-juris","lv":"reading","d":3,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Ignorantia juris non excusat”","o":["not knowing the law is no defence to breaking it","a judge unsure of the law must refer the case to a higher court","not knowing a contract's terms is no reason to escape them","not knowing the facts is no defence to breaking the law"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Ignorantia juris non excusat: not knowing the law is no defence to breaking it. Literally: ignorance of the law is no excuse. Its pair, ignorantia facti excusat: a genuine mistake of fact can be a defence. Since Kleinwort Benson v Lincoln City Council (1998), money paid under a mistake of law can be recovered in England.","about":["ignorantia-juris"],"facts":["ignorantia-juris.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ignorantia-juris-name","lv":"reading","d":4,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “Not knowing the law is no defence to breaking it”","o":["Ignorantia juris non excusat","Expressio unius est exclusio alterius","Lex posterior derogat legi priori","Ut res magis valeat quam pereat"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Ignorantia juris non excusat: not knowing the law is no defence to breaking it. Literally: ignorance of the law is no excuse. Its pair, ignorantia facti excusat: a genuine mistake of fact can be a defence. Since Kleinwort Benson v Lincoln City Council (1998), money paid under a mistake of law can be recovered in England.","about":["ignorantia-juris"],"facts":["ignorantia-juris.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-contra-proferentem","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What does this mean? “Contra proferentem” (literally: against the one who puts it forward)","o":["a late argument counts against the party that raises it","the party who brings a claim carries the whole burden of proving it","an unclear term is read against the party that put it forward","a party can't rely on its own breach to escape the contract"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Contra proferentem: an unclear term is read against the party that put it forward. Literally: against the one who puts it forward. Strongest against exclusion clauses. English courts now reach for it only once the ordinary tools of interpretation have run out, especially between commercial parties of equal strength (Persimmon Homes v Ove Arup, 2017).","about":["contra-proferentem"],"facts":["contra-proferentem.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-contra-proferentem-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “An unclear term is read against the party that put it forward”","o":["Bona fide","Contra proferentem","Audi alteram partem","Rebus sic stantibus"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Contra proferentem: an unclear term is read against the party that put it forward. Literally: against the one who puts it forward. Strongest against exclusion clauses. English courts now reach for it only once the ordinary tools of interpretation have run out, especially between commercial parties of equal strength (Persimmon Homes v Ove Arup, 2017).","about":["contra-proferentem"],"facts":["contra-proferentem.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-quantum-meruit","lv":"contract","d":5,"area":"Contracts","q":"What does this mean? “Quantum meruit” (literally: as much as he deserved)","o":["a reasonable sum for work done where no price was agreed","a bonus a court may add to damages for an especially bad breach","a fee an agent earns in proportion to the deals it brought in","damages set by the judge at what the wrongdoer can afford to pay"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Quantum meruit: a reasonable sum for work done where no price was agreed. Literally: as much as he deserved. A claim in unjust enrichment, or on an implied promise to pay a reasonable price; common where work began before a contract was signed. For goods, the Sale of Goods Act's reasonable price does the same (s 8).","about":["quantum-meruit"],"facts":["quantum-meruit.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-quantum-meruit-name","lv":"contract","d":6,"area":"Contracts","q":"Which legal term means this? “A reasonable sum for work done where no price was agreed”","o":["Caveat emptor","Ne bis in idem","Pacta sunt servanda","Quantum meruit"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Quantum meruit: a reasonable sum for work done where no price was agreed. Literally: as much as he deserved. A claim in unjust enrichment, or on an implied promise to pay a reasonable price; common where work began before a contract was signed. For goods, the Sale of Goods Act's reasonable price does the same (s 8).","about":["quantum-meruit"],"facts":["quantum-meruit.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-res-nullius","lv":"property","d":5,"area":"Property and equity","q":"What does this mean? “Res nullius” (literally: nobody's thing)","o":["a thing whose owner died without heirs, so it goes to the crown","a thing owned by no one, which becomes the first taker's","a thing that can never be owned, like the air or the open sea","a thing so worthless that no court will hear a claim for it"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Res nullius: a thing owned by no one, which becomes the first taker's. Literally: nobody's thing. Wild animals and fish, abandoned things: ownership by taking (occupatio). Its cousin terra nullius, land belonging to no state, was the excuse for many colonial claims, rejected for Australia in Mabo (1992).","about":["res-nullius"],"facts":["res-nullius.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-res-nullius-name","lv":"property","d":6,"area":"Property and equity","q":"Which legal term means this? “A thing owned by no one, which becomes the first taker's”","o":["Res nullius","Jus cogens","Ipso facto","Lex talionis"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Res nullius: a thing owned by no one, which becomes the first taker's. Literally: nobody's thing. Wild animals and fish, abandoned things: ownership by taking (occupatio). Its cousin terra nullius, land belonging to no state, was the excuse for many colonial claims, rejected for Australia in Mabo (1992).","about":["res-nullius"],"facts":["res-nullius.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-nemo-tenetur","lv":"wrongs","d":5,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"What does this mean? “Nemo tenetur se ipsum accusare” (literally: no one is bound to accuse himself)","o":["no one is bound to report a crime they saw another commit","no one is bound to answer a claim made by an unknown accuser","no one is bound to give evidence that incriminates themselves","no one is bound to pay a debt they never acknowledged in writing"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Nemo tenetur se ipsum accusare: no one is bound to give evidence that incriminates themselves. Literally: no one is bound to accuse himself. The privilege against self-incrimination and the right to silence, implied in the European Convention's fair trial (art. 6; Saunders v UK, 1996, on answers compelled by inspectors); 'pleading the Fifth' in the US.","about":["nemo-tenetur"],"facts":["nemo-tenetur.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-nemo-tenetur-name","lv":"wrongs","d":6,"area":"Wrongs and crimes","q":"Which legal term means this? “No one is bound to give evidence that incriminates themselves”","o":["Nullum crimen sine lege","Volenti non fit injuria","In dubio pro reo","Nemo tenetur se ipsum accusare"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Nemo tenetur se ipsum accusare: no one is bound to give evidence that incriminates themselves. Literally: no one is bound to accuse himself. The privilege against self-incrimination and the right to silence, implied in the European Convention's fair trial (art. 6; Saunders v UK, 1996, on answers compelled by inspectors); 'pleading the Fifth' in the US.","about":["nemo-tenetur"],"facts":["nemo-tenetur.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ratio-decidendi","lv":"disputes","d":5,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What does this mean? “Ratio decidendi” (literally: the reason for deciding)","o":["the reasons a court gives for awarding costs to one side","the judge's remarks in passing, which persuade but don't bind","the legal reasoning a decision rests on, binding later courts","the facts the court found proved, on which it based the result"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Ratio decidendi: the legal reasoning a decision rests on, binding later courts. Literally: the reason for deciding. Its opposite is obiter dictum, a thing said by the way. Finding the ratio of a case with several judgments is half of a common lawyer's training.","about":["ratio-decidendi"],"facts":["ratio-decidendi.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ratio-decidendi-name","lv":"disputes","d":6,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “The legal reasoning a decision rests on, binding later courts”","o":["Obiter dictum","Ratio decidendi","Res judicata","Pro bono"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Ratio decidendi: the legal reasoning a decision rests on, binding later courts. Literally: the reason for deciding. Its opposite is obiter dictum, a thing said by the way. Finding the ratio of a case with several judgments is half of a common lawyer's training.","about":["ratio-decidendi"],"facts":["ratio-decidendi.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-uberrimae-fidei","lv":"finance","d":5,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"What does this mean? “Uberrimae fidei” (literally: of the utmost good faith)","o":["an agent must never take a secret profit from its principal","a trustee must put the beneficiaries' interests above its own gain","both sides to any contract must deal honestly, by law","the insured must disclose every material fact before cover starts"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Uberrimae fidei: the insured must disclose every material fact before cover starts. Literally: of the utmost good faith. Marine Insurance Act 1906, ss 17 and 18. The Insurance Act 2015 turned the duty to disclose into a 'duty of fair presentation', with remedies in proportion, from 2016.","about":["uberrimae-fidei"],"facts":["uberrimae-fidei.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-uberrimae-fidei-name","lv":"finance","d":6,"area":"Paying, securing, insuring","q":"Which legal term means this? “The insured must disclose every material fact before cover starts”","o":["Ratio decidendi","Ejusdem generis","Ne bis in idem","Uberrimae fidei"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Uberrimae fidei: the insured must disclose every material fact before cover starts. Literally: of the utmost good faith. Marine Insurance Act 1906, ss 17 and 18. The Insurance Act 2015 turned the duty to disclose into a 'duty of fair presentation', with remedies in proportion, from 2016.","about":["uberrimae-fidei"],"facts":["uberrimae-fidei.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-nemo-dat","lv":"sale","d":3,"area":"Selling goods","q":"What does this mean? “Nemo dat quod non habet”","o":["a gift isn't made until the thing is handed over","a seller can't be made to deliver goods it has lost","a debtor can't be made to pay more than it owns","a buyer gets no better title than the seller itself had"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Nemo dat quod non habet: a buyer gets no better title than the seller itself had. Literally: no one gives what they do not have. The rule of the Sale of Goods Act 1979, s 21, with exceptions for estoppel, mercantile agents, sellers and buyers in possession and sales under a voidable title. Civil law protects a good-faith buyer far more (§ 932 BGB; Code civil, art. 2276).","about":["nemo-dat"],"facts":["nemo-dat.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-nemo-dat-name","lv":"sale","d":4,"area":"Selling goods","q":"Which legal term means this? “A buyer gets no better title than the seller itself had”","o":["Caveat emptor","Nemo iudex in causa sua","Pro forma","Nemo dat quod non habet"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Nemo dat quod non habet: a buyer gets no better title than the seller itself had. Literally: no one gives what they do not have. The rule of the Sale of Goods Act 1979, s 21, with exceptions for estoppel, mercantile agents, sellers and buyers in possession and sales under a voidable title. Civil law protects a good-faith buyer far more (§ 932 BGB; Code civil, art. 2276).","about":["nemo-dat"],"facts":["nemo-dat.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-in-rem","lv":"shipping","d":5,"area":"Ships and cargo","q":"What does this mean? “In rem” (literally: against the thing)","o":["a claim by a buyer to be handed the goods it has paid for","a claim against goods seized at a border for breach of customs","a claim against a ship itself, enforced by arresting it in port","a claim decided on the documents alone, with no oral hearing"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"In rem: a claim against a ship itself, enforced by arresting it in port. Literally: against the thing. The admiralty action names the ship as defendant; arrest gives the claimant security, and an owner who defends submits to the court in personam too. In civil law, a right in rem is a property right, good against everyone; one in personam binds one person.","about":["in-rem"],"facts":["in-rem.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-in-rem-name","lv":"shipping","d":6,"area":"Ships and cargo","q":"Which legal term means this? “A claim against a ship itself, enforced by arresting it in port”","o":["Pro bono","Ipso facto","Bona fide","In rem"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"In rem: a claim against a ship itself, enforced by arresting it in port. Literally: against the thing. The admiralty action names the ship as defendant; arrest gives the claimant security, and an owner who defends submits to the court in personam too. In civil law, a right in rem is a property right, good against everyone; one in personam binds one person.","about":["in-rem"],"facts":["in-rem.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-lex-specialis","lv":"reading","d":5,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Lex specialis derogat legi generali”","o":["the higher rule prevails over the lower, a constitution over a statute","the stricter rule prevails over the milder one in criminal cases","the later rule prevails over the earlier one on the same point","the rule made for the particular case prevails over the broader one"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Lex specialis derogat legi generali: the rule made for the particular case prevails over the broader one. Literally: the special law overrides the general. A canon for statutes, contracts and treaties alike: in international law, the law of armed conflict is the lex specialis beside human rights (ICJ, Nuclear Weapons, 1996). A later general law doesn't override an earlier special one: generalia specialibus non derogant.","about":["lex-specialis"],"facts":["lex-specialis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-lex-specialis-name","lv":"reading","d":6,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “The rule made for the particular case prevails over the broader one”","o":["Ubi jus, ibi remedium","Lex specialis derogat legi generali","Lex posterior derogat legi priori","De minimis non curat lex"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Lex specialis derogat legi generali: the rule made for the particular case prevails over the broader one. Literally: the special law overrides the general. A canon for statutes, contracts and treaties alike: in international law, the law of armed conflict is the lex specialis beside human rights (ICJ, Nuclear Weapons, 1996). A later general law doesn't override an earlier special one: generalia specialibus non derogant.","about":["lex-specialis"],"facts":["lex-specialis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-lex-posterior","lv":"reading","d":5,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Lex posterior derogat legi priori”","o":["the higher rule prevails over the lower, a constitution over a statute","the earlier rule prevails, as the parties relied on it first","the later rule prevails over the earlier one on the same point","the rule made for the particular case prevails over the broader one"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Lex posterior derogat legi priori: the later rule prevails over the earlier one on the same point. Literally: the later law overrides the earlier. Parliament can't bind its successors, so a later Act impliedly repeals an earlier one that conflicts with it; but a later general law doesn't override an earlier special one.","about":["lex-posterior"],"facts":["lex-posterior.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-lex-posterior-name","lv":"reading","d":6,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “The later rule prevails over the earlier one on the same point”","o":["De minimis non curat lex","Ut res magis valeat quam pereat","Ignorantia juris non excusat","Lex posterior derogat legi priori"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Lex posterior derogat legi priori: the later rule prevails over the earlier one on the same point. Literally: the later law overrides the earlier. Parliament can't bind its successors, so a later Act impliedly repeals an earlier one that conflicts with it; but a later general law doesn't override an earlier special one.","about":["lex-posterior"],"facts":["lex-posterior.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-lex-superior","lv":"reading","d":5,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Lex superior derogat legi inferiori”","o":["the ruling of the higher court prevails over the lower court's own","the higher rule prevails over the lower, a constitution over a statute","the later rule prevails over the earlier one on the same point","the rule made for the particular case prevails over the broader general one"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Lex superior derogat legi inferiori: the higher rule prevails over the lower, a constitution over a statute. Literally: the higher law overrides the lower. The hierarchy of norms, Kelsen's Stufenbau: constitution, statute, regulation, contract. In the EU, EU law takes precedence over national law (Costa v ENEL, 1964).","about":["lex-superior"],"facts":["lex-superior.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-lex-superior-name","lv":"reading","d":6,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “The higher rule prevails over the lower, a constitution over a statute”","o":["De minimis non curat lex","Ubi jus, ibi remedium","Lex specialis derogat legi generali","Lex superior derogat legi inferiori"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Lex superior derogat legi inferiori: the higher rule prevails over the lower, a constitution over a statute. Literally: the higher law overrides the lower. The hierarchy of norms, Kelsen's Stufenbau: constitution, statute, regulation, contract. In the EU, EU law takes precedence over national law (Costa v ENEL, 1964).","about":["lex-superior"],"facts":["lex-superior.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ejusdem-generis","lv":"reading","d":5,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Ejusdem generis” (literally: of the same kind)","o":["a buyer may reject goods not of the same kind as the sample","broad words after a list cover only things like those listed","parties of the same kind must be treated alike by the law","goods of the same kind may be swapped for each other on delivery"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Ejusdem generis: broad words after a list cover only things like those listed. Literally: of the same kind. In a force majeure clause, 'wars, strikes, riots or any other cause' reaches only causes of that sort, unless 'whatsoever' or the like says otherwise. A canon of construction, with noscitur a sociis and expressio unius.","about":["ejusdem-generis"],"facts":["ejusdem-generis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ejusdem-generis-name","lv":"reading","d":6,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “Broad words after a list cover only things like those listed”","o":["Per incuriam","Mutatis mutandis","Ubi jus, ibi remedium","Ejusdem generis"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Ejusdem generis: broad words after a list cover only things like those listed. Literally: of the same kind. In a force majeure clause, 'wars, strikes, riots or any other cause' reaches only causes of that sort, unless 'whatsoever' or the like says otherwise. A canon of construction, with noscitur a sociis and expressio unius.","about":["ejusdem-generis"],"facts":["ejusdem-generis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-noscitur-a-sociis","lv":"reading","d":7,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Noscitur a sociis” (literally: it is known by its companions)","o":["a person's character is judged by the company they keep","a partner is bound by whatever the other partners agree","a witness's evidence is weighed by those who back it up","a word's meaning is taken from the words that surround it"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Noscitur a sociis: a word's meaning is taken from the words that surround it. Literally: it is known by its companions. Inland Revenue v Frere (1965): 'interest, annuities or other annual payments' read together, so 'interest' meant annual interest. A canon for statutes and contracts alike.","about":["noscitur-a-sociis"],"facts":["noscitur-a-sociis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-noscitur-a-sociis-name","lv":"reading","d":8,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “A word's meaning is taken from the words that surround it”","o":["Noscitur a sociis","Per incuriam","Mutatis mutandis","Ubi jus, ibi remedium"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Noscitur a sociis: a word's meaning is taken from the words that surround it. Literally: it is known by its companions. Inland Revenue v Frere (1965): 'interest, annuities or other annual payments' read together, so 'interest' meant annual interest. A canon for statutes and contracts alike.","about":["noscitur-a-sociis"],"facts":["noscitur-a-sociis.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-expressio-unius","lv":"reading","d":7,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Expressio unius est exclusio alterius”","o":["broad words after a list cover only things like those listed","an unclear term is read against whoever drafted it or relies on it","a word's meaning is taken from the words that surround it","listing some things implies the ones not listed are left out"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Expressio unius est exclusio alterius: listing some things implies the ones not listed are left out. Literally: to name one is to exclude the other. A guide, not a rule, as lists are often written by way of example: a clause excusing delay 'by strikes and lockouts' suggests that other causes don't.","about":["expressio-unius"],"facts":["expressio-unius.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-expressio-unius-name","lv":"reading","d":8,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “Listing some things implies the ones not listed are left out”","o":["De minimis non curat lex","Expressio unius est exclusio alterius","Ubi jus, ibi remedium","Ut res magis valeat quam pereat"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Expressio unius est exclusio alterius: listing some things implies the ones not listed are left out. Literally: to name one is to exclude the other. A guide, not a rule, as lists are often written by way of example: a clause excusing delay 'by strikes and lockouts' suggests that other causes don't.","about":["expressio-unius"],"facts":["expressio-unius.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ut-res-magis-valeat","lv":"reading","d":7,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Ut res magis valeat quam pereat” (literally: that the thing may rather have effect than fail)","o":["a debtor who can't pay in full must pay as much as it can","a law that has fallen into disuse stays in force until repealed","a term is read so that it works, rather than so that it fails","a contract partly void is wholly void, unless the parties agree"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Ut res magis valeat quam pereat: a term is read so that it works, rather than so that it fails. Literally: that the thing may rather have effect than fail. The validation principle: of two readings, courts prefer the one that gives a clause effect. Treaty interpreters call it effet utile.","about":["ut-res-magis-valeat"],"facts":["ut-res-magis-valeat.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ut-res-magis-valeat-name","lv":"reading","d":8,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “A term is read so that it works, rather than so that it fails”","o":["Ignorantia juris non excusat","Expressio unius est exclusio alterius","Ubi jus, ibi remedium","Ut res magis valeat quam pereat"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Ut res magis valeat quam pereat: a term is read so that it works, rather than so that it fails. Literally: that the thing may rather have effect than fail. The validation principle: of two readings, courts prefer the one that gives a clause effect. Treaty interpreters call it effet utile.","about":["ut-res-magis-valeat"],"facts":["ut-res-magis-valeat.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-dura-lex","lv":"reading","d":3,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Dura lex, sed lex”","o":["a law must be obeyed even when it bears hard on someone","a harsh law must be read narrowly to soften its effect","a law too harsh to bear loses its force and needn't be obeyed","a law grows harder to repeal the longer it stays in force"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Dura lex, sed lex: a law must be obeyed even when it bears hard on someone. Literally: the law is harsh, but it is the law. After Ulpian in Justinian's Digest (40.9.12): 'quod quidem perquam durum est, sed ita lex scripta est'. Its counterweight in German thought is Radbruch's formula: statute yields when its injustice becomes intolerable.","about":["dura-lex"],"facts":["dura-lex.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-dura-lex-name","lv":"reading","d":4,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “A law must be obeyed even when it bears hard on someone”","o":["Ipso facto","Ubi jus, ibi remedium","Ejusdem generis","Dura lex, sed lex"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Dura lex, sed lex: a law must be obeyed even when it bears hard on someone. Literally: the law is harsh, but it is the law. After Ulpian in Justinian's Digest (40.9.12): 'quod quidem perquam durum est, sed ita lex scripta est'. Its counterweight in German thought is Radbruch's formula: statute yields when its injustice becomes intolerable.","about":["dura-lex"],"facts":["dura-lex.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ubi-jus","lv":"reading","d":5,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Ubi jus, ibi remedium”","o":["every wrong must be punished, even if no one complains","every claim must be brought where the defendant lives","every court must offer the parties a chance to settle","every legal right must have a means of enforcing it"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Ubi jus, ibi remedium: every legal right must have a means of enforcing it. Literally: where there is a right, there is a remedy. Ashby v White (1703): a voter turned away from the poll could sue, though his candidate won anyway. It's why equity grew up beside the common law.","about":["ubi-jus"],"facts":["ubi-jus.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-ubi-jus-name","lv":"reading","d":6,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “Every legal right must have a means of enforcing it”","o":["Ubi jus, ibi remedium","Dura lex, sed lex","Ignorantia juris non excusat","Noscitur a sociis"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Ubi jus, ibi remedium: every legal right must have a means of enforcing it. Literally: where there is a right, there is a remedy. Ashby v White (1703): a voter turned away from the poll could sue, though his candidate won anyway. It's why equity grew up beside the common law.","about":["ubi-jus"],"facts":["ubi-jus.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-per-incuriam","lv":"reading","d":7,"area":"Reading the law","q":"What does this mean? “Per incuriam” (literally: through lack of care)","o":["a loss caused by carelessness, which the careless party must pay","a decision taken without hearing one side, and so void","a ruling made in ignorance of a binding authority, so not binding","a ruling made in haste, which the judge may correct within a week"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Per incuriam: a ruling made in ignorance of a binding authority, so not binding. Literally: through lack of care. Young v Bristol Aeroplane (1944): the Court of Appeal may depart from its own decision given per incuriam, such as one that overlooked a statute or a binding case.","about":["per-incuriam"],"facts":["per-incuriam.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-per-incuriam-name","lv":"reading","d":8,"area":"Reading the law","q":"Which legal term means this? “A ruling made in ignorance of a binding authority, so not binding”","o":["Dura lex, sed lex","Ubi jus, ibi remedium","Mutatis mutandis","Per incuriam"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Per incuriam: a ruling made in ignorance of a binding authority, so not binding. Literally: through lack of care. Young v Bristol Aeroplane (1944): the Court of Appeal may depart from its own decision given per incuriam, such as one that overlooked a statute or a binding case.","about":["per-incuriam"],"facts":["per-incuriam.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-forum-non-conveniens","lv":"disputes","d":5,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"What does this mean? “Forum non conveniens” (literally: not a suitable forum)","o":["a court may refuse a case the parties agreed to arbitrate instead","a court may hold a hearing by video if its own building is unsuitable","a court may refuse a case brought only to delay or harass the defendant","a court may decline a case that another country's courts should hear"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Forum non conveniens: a court may decline a case that another country's courts should hear. Literally: not a suitable forum. The Spiliada (1987): an English court stays its proceedings where another forum is clearly more appropriate for the interests of the parties and the ends of justice. EU courts may not use it (Owusu v Jackson, 2005).","about":["forum-non-conveniens"],"facts":["forum-non-conveniens.meaning"]},
{"id":"LW-forum-non-conveniens-name","lv":"disputes","d":6,"area":"Courts and arbitration","q":"Which legal term means this? “A court may decline a case that another country's courts should hear”","o":["Stare decisis","Locus standi","Res judicata","Forum non conveniens"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Forum non conveniens: a court may decline a case that another country's courts should hear. Literally: not a suitable forum. The Spiliada (1987): an English court stays its proceedings where another forum is clearly more appropriate for the interests of the parties and the ends of justice. EU courts may not use it (Owusu v Jackson, 2005).","about":["forum-non-conveniens"],"facts":["forum-non-conveniens.meaning"]}
];
